window.MODULES = [{"id": "overview", "group": "Start here", "short": "How this works", "title": "Farm Health &amp; Safety Training Pack", "html": "<h1>Farm Health &amp; Safety Training Pack</h1>\n<p><strong>Truleigh Manor Farm — Summer Hay &amp; Haylage Season</strong></p>\n<p>A complete health and safety induction and training pack for a young worker (16 years old) operating tractors, telehandlers, tedders, balers, wrappers and associated equipment during haymaking and haylage.</p>\n<hr />\n<h2>Trainee details</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Field</th>\n<th>Detail</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Trainee name</td>\n<td><strong><em>_</em></strong><strong><em>_</em></strong><strong><em>_</em></strong>____</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Date of birth</td>\n<td><strong><em>_</em></strong><strong><em>_</em></strong><strong><em>_</em></strong>____</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Age at start</td>\n<td>16</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Start date</td>\n<td><strong><em>_</em></strong><strong><em>_</em></strong><strong><em>_</em></strong>____</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Supervisor / responsible person</td>\n<td><strong><em>_</em></strong><strong><em>_</em></strong><strong><em>_</em></strong>____</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Farm</td>\n<td>Truleigh Manor Farm</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>This worker is legally a \"young person\" (under 18).</strong> The law requires a specific written risk assessment that takes account of their inexperience, immaturity and lack of awareness of risk. See Module 12.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<hr />\n<h2>How to use this pack</h2>\n<p>This pack is designed to be <strong>worked through in order, with a supervisor</strong>, before the trainee operates any machinery unsupervised. It mixes reading, discussion (\"toolbox talks\"), practical demonstration and sign-off.</p>\n<p><strong>The rule is simple: read the module → discuss it with your supervisor → watch it demonstrated → do it under supervision → get signed off.</strong> No one operates a machine alone until they are signed off as competent for that machine.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>#</th>\n<th>Module</th>\n<th>What it covers</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>00</td>\n<td><a href=\"#/read/law\">Trainer's guide &amp; the law</a></td>\n<td>For the employer/supervisor: legal duties, age rules, how to run the pack</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>01</td>\n<td><a href=\"#/read/induction\">Farm induction &amp; golden rules</a></td>\n<td>First-day induction, site layout, the non-negotiable rules</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>02</td>\n<td><a href=\"#/read/safe-stop\">The big killers &amp; Safe Stop</a></td>\n<td>What actually kills people on farms, and the Safe Stop procedure</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>03</td>\n<td><a href=\"#/read/tractors\">Tractors</a></td>\n<td>Daily checks, mounting/dismounting, driving, slopes, road use, trailers</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>04</td>\n<td><a href=\"#/read/telehandlers\">Telehandlers &amp; loaders</a></td>\n<td>Lift truck law, stability, load charts, bale handling</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>05</td>\n<td><a href=\"#/read/pto\">PTO &amp; field machinery</a></td>\n<td>Tedders, mowers, balers, wrappers; guarding; clearing blockages</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>06</td>\n<td><a href=\"#/read/hay\">Hay &amp; haylage operations</a></td>\n<td>The full job, field-to-stack, bale stacking, fire &amp; heating risk</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>07</td>\n<td><a href=\"#/read/health\">Manual handling, PPE, noise, dust &amp; vibration</a></td>\n<td>Lifting, hearing, dust masks, HAVS, sun &amp; heat</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>08</td>\n<td><a href=\"#/read/site\">Site hazards</a></td>\n<td>Working at height, electricity &amp; overhead lines, fire, slurry/confined spaces, livestock, chemicals</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>09</td>\n<td><a href=\"#/read/emergencies\">Emergencies, first aid &amp; reporting</a></td>\n<td>What to do in an accident, who to call, RIDDOR, near-miss reporting</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>10</td>\n<td><a href=\"#/read/overview\">Knowledge check (quiz)</a></td>\n<td>40-question assessment the trainee completes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>11</td>\n<td><a href=\"#/read/overview\">Answer key</a></td>\n<td>For the supervisor to mark</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>12</td>\n<td><a href=\"#/read/risk\">Young person risk assessment template</a></td>\n<td>The legally required assessment — fill in before work starts</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>13</td>\n<td><a href=\"#/read/overview\">Practical competency checklist &amp; sign-off</a></td>\n<td>Per-machine demonstration and sign-off record</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>14</td>\n<td><a href=\"#/read/overview\">Training record &amp; certificate</a></td>\n<td>Proof of completion to keep on file</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>15</td>\n<td><a href=\"#/read/hb-index\">Machinery handbook</a></td>\n<td>Safe operation &amp; maintenance for our specific kit (tractors, telehandlers, tedder, rake, baler, mowers, attachments, trailers) — incl. fuelling &amp; greasing</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>16</td>\n<td><a href=\"#/read/map\">Farm map &amp; field names</a></td>\n<td>The farm map, all 33 field names, and the hazard-markup exercise</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>—</td>\n<td><a href=\"#/read/emergency-card\">Emergency Information Card</a></td>\n<td>Print &amp; keep in the cab: 999 info, what3words, emergency contacts</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<hr />\n<h2>A note on what this pack is and isn't</h2>\n<p>This pack is built on current <strong>UK Health and Safety Executive (HSE)</strong> guidance for agriculture and young people, plus recognised good practice. It is a strong, practical foundation — but it does <strong>not</strong> replace:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Accredited operator training</strong> (e.g. <strong>Lantra</strong> or <strong>City &amp; Guilds NPTC</strong>) for telehandlers/rough-terrain lift trucks, ATVs, chainsaws and pesticide application. These should be booked where the trainee will use that kit.</li>\n<li>A <strong>site-specific risk assessment</strong> for Truleigh Manor Farm (Module 12 is your template).</li>\n<li>The <strong>operator's manual</strong> for each individual machine — always the final word on that machine.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Keep the completed pack on file. It is your evidence that you provided information, instruction, training and supervision as the law requires.</p>\n<p>See <a href=\"#/read/sources\">SOURCES.md</a> for the guidance this pack is based on.</p>"}, {"id": "law", "group": "Start here", "short": "The law & your duties", "title": "Module 00 — Trainer's Guide &amp; The Law", "html": "<h1>Module 00 — Trainer's Guide &amp; The Law</h1>\n<p><em>For the employer / supervisor. Read this before you start the trainee on the pack.</em></p>\n<hr />\n<h2>1. Your legal duties as the employer</h2>\n<p>If a young person works on your farm — paid, unpaid, family member or not — you have legal duties under:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Health and Safety at Work etc. Act 1974</strong> — the general duty to ensure, so far as reasonably practicable, the health, safety and welfare of workers.</li>\n<li><strong>Management of Health and Safety at Work Regulations 1999, regulation 19</strong> — the specific duty regarding <strong>young persons (under 18)</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Prevention of Accidents to Children in Agriculture Regulations 1998</strong> — age limits for riding on/driving machines.</li>\n<li><strong>Provision and Use of Work Equipment Regulations 1998 (PUWER)</strong> — equipment must be suitable, maintained, guarded, and only used by people who have had adequate training.</li>\n<li><strong>Lifting Operations and Lifting Equipment Regulations 1998 (LOLER)</strong> — for lifting (telehandler forks, bale grabs, etc.).</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>The young-person rule that matters most</h3>\n<p>Before the young person starts work you <strong>must</strong> carry out (and act on) a <strong>risk assessment that specifically takes account of</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>their <strong>inexperience</strong>, <strong>immaturity</strong> and <strong>lack of awareness</strong> of risks;</li>\n<li>the work, equipment and substances involved;</li>\n<li>the layout of the workplace;</li>\n<li>the extent of health and safety training to be provided.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A blank template is provided in <strong>Module 12</strong> — completing it is not optional.</p>\n<hr />\n<h2>2. Age rules — what a person of each age may legally do</h2>\n<blockquote>\n<p>These are legal <em>minimums</em>. Being old enough is not the same as being competent. Competence = training + supervision + demonstrated ability. The width/road rules below apply to <strong>public roads</strong>; on private land the duty is to be trained, competent and adequately supervised.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Activity</th>\n<th>Under 13</th>\n<th>13–15</th>\n<th>16</th>\n<th>17</th>\n<th>18+</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Ride on / drive a tractor or self-propelled machine</td>\n<td><strong>No (illegal)</strong></td>\n<td>Drive on private land <strong>only after certified training</strong></td>\n<td>Yes (trained)</td>\n<td>Yes</td>\n<td>Yes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Drive a tractor on a <strong>public road</strong></td>\n<td>No</td>\n<td>No</td>\n<td>Yes — <strong>Category F licence</strong>; tractor <strong>≤ 2.45 m wide</strong>, trailer ≤ 2.45 m wide (2 wheels, or 4 close-coupled)</td>\n<td>Yes — width limits lifted</td>\n<td>Yes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Operate <strong>balers, harvesters, mowers, tedders, PTO-driven machines, processors</strong></td>\n<td><strong>No (illegal)</strong></td>\n<td><strong>No (illegal)</strong></td>\n<td>Yes, <strong>if trained, competent and supervised</strong></td>\n<td>Yes</td>\n<td>Yes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Operate a <strong>lift truck / telehandler</strong></td>\n<td><strong>No (illegal)</strong></td>\n<td><strong>No (illegal)</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Private land only</strong>, and <strong>never</strong> without training, competence, maturity <strong>and</strong> adequate supervision</td>\n<td>As 16, plus may take the test for road use</td>\n<td>Yes (trained/certified)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Drive a telehandler <strong>on a public road</strong></td>\n<td>No</td>\n<td>No</td>\n<td>No</td>\n<td>Cat B test possible at 17; in practice usually <strong>21</strong> for insurance/road use</td>\n<td>Yes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Use <strong>ATV / quad bike</strong></td>\n<td><strong>No (illegal)</strong></td>\n<td><strong>No (illegal)</strong></td>\n<td>Yes, with appropriate training</td>\n<td>Yes</td>\n<td>Yes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Apply <strong>pesticides</strong></td>\n<td>No</td>\n<td>No</td>\n<td>Generally <strong>16+</strong> with a recognised certificate of competence</td>\n<td>Yes</td>\n<td>Yes</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Bottom line for a 16-year-old at Truleigh Manor:</strong> they may legally drive tractors and operate field machinery (tedders, mowers, balers, wrappers) and a telehandler on the farm's own land <strong>provided they are properly trained, demonstrably competent, and supervised to a degree that matches their experience.</strong> Telehandler and ATV use should be backed by <strong>accredited (Lantra/NPTC) training</strong>.</p>\n<hr />\n<h2>3. The level of supervision</h2>\n<p>Supervision is not \"in the same field somewhere.\" For a young, inexperienced worker it means, in the early stages:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Direct supervision</strong> (supervisor present and watching) for any new machine or new task;</li>\n<li>progressing to <strong>general supervision</strong> (checked frequently, can summon help) only once competence is signed off;</li>\n<li><strong>never</strong> lone working on high-risk tasks until experienced — and arguably not for telehandler/bale-stacking work for some time.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Match the supervision to the task and the person, and <strong>write down</strong> in the risk assessment what level applies to what.</p>\n<hr />\n<h2>4. How to run this pack</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Complete Module 12 (risk assessment) first</strong> — before any work.</li>\n<li>Do <strong>Module 01 (induction)</strong> on day one, on site, walking the actual farm.</li>\n<li>Work through <strong>Modules 02–09</strong> as classroom/yard sessions mixed with practical demonstration. Don't rush — a module a session is fine.</li>\n<li>For each machine, use <strong>Module 13</strong> to demonstrate, observe, and sign off competence <strong>before</strong> unsupervised use.</li>\n<li>Have the trainee complete the <strong>Module 10 quiz</strong>; mark it with <strong>Module 11</strong>. Aim for 100% on the safety-critical questions (Safe Stop, PTO, telehandler stability, overhead lines, emergencies) — re-teach anything missed.</li>\n<li>Complete <strong>Module 14 (training record &amp; certificate)</strong> and keep the whole pack on file.</li>\n<li><strong>Refresh</strong> key points regularly — a 5-minute toolbox talk at the start of busy days.</li>\n</ol>\n<hr />\n<h2>5. Records to keep on file</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Completed Module 12 risk assessment (reviewed if anything changes).</li>\n<li>Completed Module 13 competency sign-offs.</li>\n<li>Marked Module 10 quiz.</li>\n<li>Module 14 training record + certificate.</li>\n<li>Copies of any external certificates (Lantra/NPTC telehandler, ATV, etc.).</li>\n<li>Employer's Liability insurance certificate (check young/family workers are covered).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>These records are what you produce if HSE ever asks, or if there is an incident.</p>"}, {"id": "induction", "group": "Core safety", "short": "Induction & golden rules", "title": "Module 01 — Farm Induction &amp; Golden Rules", "html": "<h1>Module 01 — Farm Induction &amp; Golden Rules</h1>\n<p><em>Do this on day one, on site, walking the actual farm with your supervisor.</em></p>\n<hr />\n<h2>Welcome — why this matters</h2>\n<p>Farming is one of the <strong>most dangerous jobs in the UK</strong>. Agriculture has a fatal injury rate around <strong>21 times higher</strong> than the average across all industries. Most people who are killed or seriously hurt were doing a routine job they'd done many times. The difference between a normal day and a disaster is usually one rushed decision, one skipped check, or one moment standing in the wrong place.</p>\n<p>You are 16. You are legally allowed to do this work, but you are new to it. <strong>There is no job on this farm so urgent that it's worth your life or your hands.</strong> If you are ever unsure, <strong>stop and ask.</strong> Nobody will think less of you for asking — but everybody will care if you get hurt.</p>\n<hr />\n<h2>Your right to a safe day</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>You can <strong>say no</strong> to a task you haven't been trained for.</li>\n<li>You can <strong>stop work</strong> if you think something is unsafe.</li>\n<li>You can <strong>ask questions</strong> as many times as you need.</li>\n<li>You will be <strong>supervised</strong> until you are signed off as competent — that is normal and required.</li>\n</ul>\n<hr />\n<h2>The induction walk-round (tick as you go)</h2>\n<p>Your supervisor will walk you round and show you:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>☐ The farm layout: fields, yard, tracks, gateways, the route hay/haylage takes from field to stack.</li>\n<li>☐ Where vehicles and machines move, and where pedestrians are safe to stand/walk.</li>\n<li>☐ Overhead power lines and their routes (<strong>mark them on the <a href=\"#/read/map\">farm map — Module 16</a></strong> as part of the markup exercise).</li>\n<li>☐ The <strong>field names and layout</strong> (<a href=\"#/read/map\">Module 16</a>) — so you can say exactly where you are.</li>\n<li>☐ Slopes, ditches, ponds, soft ground, and known tipping/overturn risks (e.g. Sands Hill, The Hill, Banky Sands, The Lakes, The Brooks).</li>\n<li>☐ The fuel store, chemical/pesticide store, and any gas/oxy-acetylene.</li>\n<li>☐ Where the <strong>first aid kit(s)</strong> are, and where the <strong>eye wash</strong> is.</li>\n<li>☐ Where the <strong>fire extinguishers</strong> are (in buildings and on machines).</li>\n<li>☐ The <strong>assembly point</strong> if there's a fire or emergency.</li>\n<li>☐ The farm's <strong>address and what3words</strong> for calling 999 (also on the <a href=\"#/read/emergency-card\">Emergency Information Card</a> and Module 09):</li>\n<li><strong>Truleigh Manor Farm, Edburton Road, Henfield, BN5 9LL</strong></li>\n<li>Farm entrance: <code>///chicken.airless.hazy</code> · Truleigh Sands: <code>///playback.poker.touched</code></li>\n<li>☐ Welfare: toilet, hand-washing, drinking water, breaks, somewhere to shelter.</li>\n<li>☐ Who your supervisor is, and how to contact them at any moment (save these numbers): <strong>Ben Hogan 07876 683949</strong>, <strong>Sandra Windus 07901 521832</strong>, <strong>Office 01903 816065</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<hr />\n<h2>The Golden Rules (non-negotiable)</h2>\n<p>These apply to everyone, every day, no exceptions:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>SAFE STOP before you leave the seat or anyone approaches.</strong> Handbrake on → controls in neutral → engine off → key out. (Full detail in Module 02.)</li>\n<li><strong>Never clear a blockage, adjust or touch a machine while the engine is running or the PTO is turning.</strong> Stop it. Wait for ALL moving parts to stop.</li>\n<li><strong>No passengers</strong> on tractors, telehandlers or in/on trailers unless there is a proper, fitted instructor seat. Never ride on a load, drawbar, linkage or in a raised bucket.</li>\n<li><strong>Never go under a raised load, raised trailer body, or raised loader/forks</strong> that isn't physically propped/locked. Hydraulics can fail.</li>\n<li><strong>Stay clear of the PTO shaft</strong> and all guarded moving parts. Tie up loose clothing, no dangling cords, no loose long hair, no jewellery.</li>\n<li><strong>Wear the right PPE</strong> for the job: steel-toe boots always; ear and dust protection in noise/dust; gloves, eye protection and hi-vis as needed.</li>\n<li><strong>Mind overhead power lines</strong> — telehandlers, tipping trailers and high loads can reach them. Know where they are.</li>\n<li><strong>Don't use a machine you haven't been trained and signed off on.</strong> Ask.</li>\n<li><strong>Phone:</strong> never use it while driving/operating. Pull over, Safe Stop, then use it.</li>\n<li><strong>If you're tired, ill, or it's getting dark and you're rushing — stop.</strong> Fatigue and \"just one more load\" cause accidents. Tell your supervisor.</li>\n<li><strong>No alcohol or drugs.</strong> They have no place anywhere near this machinery.</li>\n<li><strong>If in doubt — STOP and ASK.</strong></li>\n</ol>\n<hr />\n<h2>Discussion (do this out loud with your supervisor)</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Point to where the nearest first aid kit and fire extinguisher are, right now.</li>\n<li>Say the full address and postcode you'd give to 999.</li>\n<li>Name three places on this farm where a machine could tip over.</li>\n<li>Where are the overhead power lines?</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Sign-off:</strong> Trainee has completed the induction walk-round and understands the Golden Rules.</p>\n<p>Trainee signature: <strong><em>_</em></strong><strong><em>_</em></strong><strong><em>_  Date: </em></strong><strong><em>_</em></strong></p>\n<p>Supervisor signature: <strong><em>_</em></strong><strong><em>_</em></strong><strong><em>_  Date: </em></strong><strong><em>_</em></strong></p>"}, {"id": "safe-stop", "group": "Core safety", "short": "Big killers & Safe Stop", "title": "Module 02 — The Big Killers &amp; Safe Stop", "html": "<h1>Module 02 — The Big Killers &amp; Safe Stop</h1>\n<p><em>The single most important module. If you remember nothing else, remember Safe Stop.</em></p>\n<hr />\n<h2>What actually kills and injures people on farms</h2>\n<p>Across recent years, HSE's fatal injury data for agriculture shows the same causes coming up again and again. In the 2023/24 reporting year there were around <strong>23 agriculture-related deaths</strong>. The main categories were:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Cause</th>\n<th>What it looks like in real life</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Transport</strong> (the biggest)</td>\n<td>Being <strong>run over</strong> by a tractor/telehandler, vehicles <strong>overturning</strong> on slopes, people <strong>crushed</strong> against walls/gates, runaway machines</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Struck by moving/falling objects</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Falling bales</strong>, collapsing stacks, loads dropping from loaders, raised trailer bodies coming down</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Livestock</strong></td>\n<td>Crushed, knocked down or trampled (especially cattle, and cows with calves)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Machinery contact</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Entanglement in PTO shafts</strong> and moving parts; reaching into running machines to clear blockages</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Falls from height</strong></td>\n<td>Falling from machines, trailers, stacks, fragile roofs; falls while working off a telehandler</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Notice the pattern:</strong> almost all of these involve a machine that was <em>moving or could move</em>, or a load that could <em>fall</em>. And a large share happen during a quick \"I'll just sort that\" moment — clearing a blockage, hopping off to open a gate, nipping under a raised body.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>HSE has found that around 60% of all \"run over\" deaths on farms could have been prevented simply by putting the handbrake on.</strong> That is how powerful one habit is.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<hr />\n<h2>SAFE STOP — the four steps</h2>\n<p>Do this <strong>every single time</strong> before you leave the operating position, before anyone approaches the machine, and before any adjustment, maintenance or blockage clearing:</p>\n<h3>1. Handbrake ON</h3>\n<h3>2. Controls in NEUTRAL (and lower any raised attachment to the ground)</h3>\n<h3>3. Engine OFF</h3>\n<h3>4. Key OUT (and pocket it — or lock off the power)</h3>\n<p>A simple way to remember it: <strong>\"Brake — Neutral — Off — Out.\"</strong></p>\n<p>If you're getting off even for ten seconds to open a gate, pick something up, or talk to someone: <strong>Safe Stop.</strong> Machines have rolled and killed people in the time it takes to walk round to the back.</p>\n<hr />\n<h2>When you MUST Safe Stop</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Before getting off the seat for <strong>any</strong> reason.</li>\n<li>Before <strong>anyone</strong> approaches the machine or steps near moving parts.</li>\n<li>Before clearing a <strong>blockage</strong> (baler, mower, tedder — anything).</li>\n<li>Before any <strong>adjustment, greasing, refuelling or maintenance</strong>.</li>\n<li>Before checking, touching, or photographing anything on the machine.</li>\n<li>Before walking away from it — even \"just for a minute.\"</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>The deadly shortcut to never take</h2>\n<p>The classic fatal accident: a machine blocks, the operator leaves it running (\"it'll only take a second\"), reaches in, and a sleeve, glove or hand is caught by a still-turning part. <strong>PTO shafts spin fast and have no mercy.</strong> Moving parts can keep turning for several seconds after you disengage drive. <strong>Stop everything. Wait until ALL movement has completely stopped. Then clear it.</strong></p>\n<hr />\n<h2>Parking up safely</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Park on <strong>firm, level ground</strong> where you can.</li>\n<li>Lower all attachments (loader, forks, linkage) <strong>to the ground</strong>.</li>\n<li>Handbrake on, in gear/park as the manual says, chock wheels if on any slope.</li>\n<li>Never leave a machine <strong>running and unattended</strong>.</li>\n<li>Never leave a machine where it could roll, or where children could reach it.</li>\n</ul>\n<hr />\n<h2>Discussion / demonstration</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Recite the four Safe Stop steps from memory.</li>\n<li>Demonstrate Safe Stop on a tractor to your supervisor.</li>\n<li>Explain <em>why</em> you wait for moving parts to stop before clearing a blockage, even after disengaging the PTO.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Sign-off:</strong> Trainee can recite and correctly demonstrate Safe Stop.</p>\n<p>Trainee signature: <strong><em>_</em></strong><strong><em>_</em></strong><strong><em>_  Supervisor signature: </em></strong><strong><em>_</em></strong><strong><em>_</em></strong>_  Date: <strong><em>_</em></strong>___</p>"}, {"id": "tractors", "group": "Core safety", "short": "Tractors", "title": "Module 03 — Tractors", "html": "<h1>Module 03 — Tractors</h1>\n<p><em>Tractors are the most-used machine on the farm and involved in the most deaths. Respect them.</em></p>\n<hr />\n<h2>Before you drive: the law for a 16-year-old</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>On <strong>private farmland</strong>: you may drive a tractor at 16 once you are <strong>trained and competent</strong>, and while you are <strong>supervised</strong> to a level that matches your experience.</li>\n<li>On a <strong>public road</strong>: you need a <strong>Category F licence</strong> (you can get this at 16). At 16 you may only drive a tractor <strong>up to 2.45 m wide</strong>, towing a trailer up to 2.45 m wide (two wheels, or four close-coupled wheels). The width restriction lifts at 17.</li>\n<li>Always wear the <strong>seatbelt</strong> if one is fitted — it keeps you within the protective cab/frame (ROPS) if the tractor overturns.</li>\n</ul>\n<hr />\n<h2>Daily pre-start checks (\"POWER\" + walk-round)</h2>\n<p>Walk around the tractor before you start. A quick check catches most problems:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>☐ <strong>Tyres</strong> — condition and pressure, no big cuts, wheel nuts present.</li>\n<li>☐ <strong>Fuel &amp; fluids</strong> — diesel, engine oil, coolant, hydraulic oil; look for leaks under the tractor.</li>\n<li>☐ <strong>Lights, beacon, indicators, brake lights, mirrors</strong> — working and clean.</li>\n<li>☐ <strong>Guards</strong> — PTO shaft guard and all guards present and secure.</li>\n<li>☐ <strong>Cab</strong> — clean glass, working wipers, nothing loose rolling around the floor, fire extinguisher present.</li>\n<li>☐ <strong>Steps and handholds</strong> — clean of mud so you don't slip.</li>\n<li>☐ <strong>Linkage / drawbar / pickup hitch</strong> — sound, pins and clips in place.</li>\n<li>☐ <strong>Brakes</strong> — including that the <strong>brake pedals are latched together</strong> for road work.</li>\n<li>☐ <strong>Seatbelt</strong> — present and working.</li>\n<li>☐ <strong>Controls</strong> — know where everything is <em>before</em> you move.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Report anything wrong to your supervisor. Don't use a defective tractor.</p>\n<hr />\n<h2>Getting on and off — the right way</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Use the <strong>steps and handholds</strong>, always <strong>three points of contact</strong> (two hands + one foot, or two feet + one hand).</li>\n<li>Face the tractor when climbing down. <strong>Never jump down</strong> — twisted ankles, slips and falls are common.</li>\n<li>Keep steps clean of mud.</li>\n<li><strong>Never get on or off a moving tractor.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<hr />\n<h2>Driving safely</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Seatbelt on, doors closed.</strong></li>\n<li>Start only from the <strong>driver's seat</strong>, in neutral, PTO off. Never start by bridging the starter from the ground — people are crushed every year by tractors starting in gear.</li>\n<li>Keep speed <strong>sensible for the conditions</strong> — wet grass, slopes, loads and the yard all need slowing down.</li>\n<li>Watch your <strong>width and length</strong>, especially with mounted/towed kit and in gateways.</li>\n<li><strong>No passengers</strong> unless there's a proper instructor seat with a seatbelt.</li>\n<li>Take corners slowly; a raised load or full trailer changes how the tractor handles.</li>\n<li>In the yard, assume someone could step out — go slow, use the beacon, sound the horn at blind corners.</li>\n</ul>\n<hr />\n<h2>Slopes and overturning — a top killer</h2>\n<p>Tractors <strong>overturn sideways</strong> and <strong>roll backwards</strong>. Both kill.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Know the ground. <strong>Drive up and down slopes, not across</strong>, where possible.</li>\n<li>Slow right down on slopes; <strong>never change direction sharply</strong> on a slope.</li>\n<li>Be very careful near <strong>ditches, banks and soft edges</strong> — a wheel dropping in can flip the tractor.</li>\n<li>Keep loaders and mounted implements <strong>low</strong> when moving — a high load raises the centre of gravity and makes tipping far easier.</li>\n<li>Pulling a stuck load: hitch only to the <strong>drawbar/proper hitch point at the correct height</strong> — never high up. Hitching too high causes the tractor to rear up and flip over backwards.</li>\n<li>If the tractor starts to tip and you have a cab/ROPS and seatbelt: <strong>stay in the seat, hold on, lean away from the direction of fall.</strong> Jumping out usually means being crushed under it.</li>\n</ul>\n<hr />\n<h2>Trailers</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Match trailer and load to the tractor; don't overload.</li>\n<li>Make sure the <strong>brakes and lights</strong> on the trailer work.</li>\n<li>Check the <strong>hitch is locked</strong> and the safety pin/clip is in.</li>\n<li>Connect the <strong>brake line and breakaway/safety chain</strong>.</li>\n<li>Never stand between tractor and trailer while hitching with the engine running.</li>\n<li>Load evenly and within limits; secure loose loads.</li>\n<li><strong>Never let anyone ride in or on a trailer.</strong></li>\n<li>When tipping: tip only on <strong>firm, level ground</strong>, watch for <strong>overhead power lines</strong>, and <strong>never go under a raised body</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<hr />\n<h2>Discussion / demonstration</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Carry out a full pre-start walk-round and tell your supervisor what you're checking and why.</li>\n<li>Mount and dismount correctly (three points of contact).</li>\n<li>Explain why you drive up/down a slope rather than across, and why you never hitch high.</li>\n<li>Demonstrate hitching a trailer and connecting brakes/lights/safety chain.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Sign-off:</strong> Trainee can safely check, mount, drive, and hitch a tractor under supervision.</p>\n<p>Trainee signature: <strong><em>_</em></strong><strong><em>_</em></strong><strong><em>_  Supervisor signature: </em></strong><strong><em>_</em></strong><strong><em>_</em></strong>_  Date: <strong><em>_</em></strong>___</p>"}, {"id": "telehandlers", "group": "Core safety", "short": "Telehandlers & loaders", "title": "Module 04 — Telehandlers &amp; Loaders", "html": "<h1>Module 04 — Telehandlers &amp; Loaders</h1>\n<p><em>Telehandlers are involved in serious crush, overturn, falling-load and overhead-line deaths every year. This is high-risk work — treat the training seriously.</em></p>\n<hr />\n<h2>The law and training — read this first</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>A telehandler is a <strong>rough-terrain lift truck</strong>. Lift trucks are covered by the HSE <strong>Approved Code of Practice \"Rideable lift trucks\"</strong> and by <strong>PUWER</strong> and <strong>LOLER</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Anyone under 16 must never operate a telehandler.</strong></li>\n<li>A <strong>young person (under 18)</strong> must <strong>not</strong> operate one unless they have the <strong>necessary competence and maturity</strong> <em>and</em> have completed <strong>appropriate, accredited training</strong> <em>and</em> are <strong>adequately supervised</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>On a public road</strong>, telehandlers are treated as agricultural vehicles needing a car (Category B) licence — possible from 17, but in practice most operators are <strong>21+</strong> for road and insurance reasons. <strong>Assume road use is not for a 16-year-old.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Action for Truleigh Manor:</strong> book <strong>accredited operator training (Lantra or NPTC)</strong> before the trainee uses the telehandler. This pack prepares them for it and reinforces it — it does not replace it. Early use must be <strong>directly supervised</strong>.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<hr />\n<h2>Why telehandlers are dangerous</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>They are <strong>heavy and can tip</strong> — sideways or forwards — especially with a raised, extended or heavy load.</li>\n<li>The <strong>boom reaches up and out</strong>, so it can hit <strong>overhead power lines</strong> and unbalance the machine.</li>\n<li><strong>Loads can fall</strong> — bales especially — onto people nearby.</li>\n<li>People get <strong>crushed</strong> between the machine and walls/gates, or struck while it reverses.</li>\n<li>Using one as a <strong>work platform</strong> (lifting people on the forks or in an unapproved cage) has killed people — <strong>never lift a person except in a proper, tested man-basket designed and rated for that telehandler</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<hr />\n<h2>Pre-use checks</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>☐ Tyres, condition and pressure.</li>\n<li>☐ Hydraulic hoses and rams — no leaks, no damage.</li>\n<li>☐ Boom, forks/attachment, and the <strong>quick-hitch locked and pinned</strong>.</li>\n<li>☐ Load chart / <strong>rated capacity indicator</strong> present and working.</li>\n<li>☐ Brakes, steering, lights, beacon, horn, reversing alarm, mirrors/camera.</li>\n<li>☐ Seatbelt — <strong>wear it</strong>.</li>\n<li>☐ Cab clean, controls understood, nothing loose.</li>\n<li>☐ Attachment correct for the job (bale spike/grab for round bales — <strong>not the bucket</strong>).</li>\n</ul>\n<hr />\n<h2>Stability — the rules that keep it upright</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Know the load chart.</strong> Capacity drops sharply as you raise and extend the boom. Stay within it — never guess.</li>\n<li><strong>Travel with the load LOW</strong> and the boom retracted, tilted back. A raised load while driving is how telehandlers tip.</li>\n<li><strong>Travel and turn slowly.</strong> Most overturns happen turning with a raised load or on a slope.</li>\n<li>On slopes: keep the <strong>load on the uphill side</strong>, travel up/down rather than across, and keep the load low.</li>\n<li>Use the machine on <strong>firm, level ground</strong> for lifting. Soft or uneven ground can let it tip.</li>\n<li><strong>Don't exceed the reach</strong> — extending forward shifts the centre of gravity past the front wheels.</li>\n<li>If fitted, watch the <strong>longitudinal stability indicator</strong> and stop before it warns.</li>\n</ul>\n<hr />\n<h2>Loading, lifting and placing</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Approach the load square, forks level, at the right width.</li>\n<li>Lift smoothly; tilt back slightly to cradle the load.</li>\n<li>Make sure the load is <strong>stable and won't roll/slide</strong> before you move.</li>\n<li>Keep <strong>people well clear</strong> — nobody under a raised load or in the swing/travel path, ever.</li>\n<li>Place loads on <strong>firm, level ground</strong> and build stacks square (see Module 06 for bale stacking).</li>\n<li>Lower the boom fully and tilt forks down to release; reverse out straight.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Overhead power lines — a telehandler special</h2>\n<p>The raised boom can reach lines you'd never touch with a tractor. <strong>Electricity can jump (arc) to the boom without touching it.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Know where every overhead line on the farm runs (Module 08).</li>\n<li><strong>Never raise the boom near lines.</strong> Plan stacking and tipping away from them.</li>\n<li>If you ever contact a line: <strong>stay in the cab</strong>, lower/reverse clear if you can, and <strong>don't let anyone touch the machine</strong>. If you must get out (e.g. fire), <strong>jump clear with both feet together and don't touch machine and ground at the same time</strong>, then shuffle away.</li>\n</ul>\n<hr />\n<h2>Getting on and off / parking</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Three points of contact, face the machine, never jump.</li>\n<li>Park on level ground, <strong>forks/attachment flat on the ground</strong>, boom retracted, Safe Stop (brake, neutral, off, key out).</li>\n<li>Never leave it with the boom raised or a load up.</li>\n</ul>\n<hr />\n<h2>Discussion / demonstration (then accredited training)</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Read the <strong>load chart</strong> and explain how capacity changes as the boom raises and extends.</li>\n<li>Explain why you travel with the load low.</li>\n<li>Show where the overhead lines are and how you'd plan a stacking job to avoid them.</li>\n<li>Demonstrate (supervised) picking up, carrying low, and placing a round bale with the correct attachment.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Sign-off (interim — full sign-off requires accredited certificate):</strong></p>\n<p>Trainee signature: <strong><em>_</em></strong><strong><em>_</em></strong><strong><em>_  Supervisor signature: </em></strong><strong><em>_</em></strong><strong><em>_</em></strong>_  Date: <strong><em>_</em></strong>___</p>\n<p>Accredited (Lantra/NPTC) telehandler certificate obtained?  ☐ Yes — date: <strong><em>_</em></strong><em>  ☐ Booked: <strong><em>_</em></strong></em></p>"}, {"id": "pto", "group": "Core safety", "short": "PTO & field machinery", "title": "Module 05 — PTO &amp; Field Machinery (Tedders, Mowers, Balers, Wrappers)", "html": "<h1>Module 05 — PTO &amp; Field Machinery (Tedders, Mowers, Balers, Wrappers)</h1>\n<p><em>This module covers the spinning, cutting, gathering machines used to make hay and haylage. They are unforgiving. The rule is: stop everything before you go near it.</em></p>\n<hr />\n<h2>The PTO shaft — treat it like a live danger</h2>\n<p>The <strong>Power Take-Off (PTO)</strong> shaft transfers power from the tractor to the machine. It spins at high speed (typically 540 or 1000 rpm). People are <strong>entangled and killed or dismembered</strong> by PTO shafts every year — usually after their clothing catches.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The PTO shaft <strong>must have its full guard</strong> fitted, in good condition, and the <strong>anti-rotation chains</strong> attached at both ends so the guard doesn't spin. <strong>Never run a machine with a damaged or missing PTO guard.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Never step over, lean over, or reach across</strong> a turning PTO shaft.</li>\n<li><strong>Tie up loose clothing.</strong> No dangling cords, scarves, untucked shirts, loose sleeves. No jewellery. Long hair tied up and tucked away. A loose drawstring is all it takes.</li>\n<li><strong>Disengage the PTO and Safe Stop before connecting/disconnecting</strong> the shaft or going anywhere near it.</li>\n<li>Connect the PTO with the <strong>engine off</strong>; make sure it's locked on at both ends.</li>\n</ul>\n<hr />\n<h2>Guards — never defeat them</h2>\n<p>Every guard on every machine is there because someone was hurt without it.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Guards must be <strong>in place and secure</strong> before the machine runs.</li>\n<li><strong>Never remove, tie back, or wedge open a guard</strong> to make a job easier.</li>\n<li>If a guard is <strong>missing or broken, take the machine out of use</strong> and tell your supervisor.</li>\n<li>Replace guards <strong>before</strong> restarting after any maintenance.</li>\n</ul>\n<hr />\n<h2>Clearing blockages — the rule that saves hands and lives</h2>\n<p>Blockages are the <strong>most common reason people reach into running machines</strong> — and one of the most common ways they're killed.</p>\n<p><strong>The procedure, every time:</strong>\n1. <strong>Safe Stop</strong> — handbrake, neutral, PTO <strong>disengaged</strong>, engine <strong>off</strong>, key <strong>out and in your pocket</strong>.\n2. <strong>Wait</strong> until <strong>all</strong> moving parts have <strong>completely stopped</strong> — flywheels, rotors and tines coast on for several seconds.\n3. Only then clear the blockage, using a <strong>tool, not your hands</strong> where possible.\n4. Watch for <strong>stored energy</strong> — tensioned springs, raised parts, hydraulic pressure, a pick-up reel under load can spring back. Release/secure it safely.\n5. Replace any guard, stand clear, then restart.</p>\n<p><strong>Never</strong> clear a blockage with the engine running. <strong>Never</strong> ask, or let anyone, hold/feed material while the machine runs.</p>\n<hr />\n<h2>Tedders &amp; rakes</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Fast-spinning tines fling debris — keep people clear and wear <strong>eye protection</strong>.</li>\n<li>Tines are sharp and the rotors carry momentum — <strong>wait for them to stop</strong> before touching.</li>\n<li>Fold/unfold the wings exactly as the manual says; keep clear of the folding path (crush points).</li>\n<li>Watch width on the road and through gateways.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Mowers (disc/drum mowers, conditioners)</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Blades are razor-sharp and spin extremely fast; they can <strong>throw stones and broken blade pieces</strong> a long way — keep bystanders well back and never stand in front.</li>\n<li>Check blades/skids before use; replace damaged blades as a set.</li>\n<li>The mower <strong>coasts</strong> after the PTO stops — wait fully before approaching.</li>\n<li>Engage the <strong>breakback/safety device</strong> correctly when transporting.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Balers (round and conventional)</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>The <strong>pick-up, rollers, knotter/wrapping mechanism and chamber</strong> are all entanglement and crush hazards. <strong>Never</strong> reach into the pick-up or chamber while anything moves.</li>\n<li><strong>Bale ejection:</strong> the tailgate and ejected bale can strike/crush. <strong>Stand clear of the rear</strong> when ejecting; make sure no one is behind.</li>\n<li>On slopes, balers are tall with a <strong>high centre of gravity</strong> — go slow, avoid holes and drop-offs, never turn sharply across a slope.</li>\n<li>Twine/net wrap: thread it with the machine <strong>stopped</strong>. Don't feed it by hand while running.</li>\n<li>Fire risk: balers run hot and dusty — see Module 06.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Bale wrappers (for haylage)</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>The <strong>rotating ring/arms and table move fast and unpredictably</strong> — stay outside the working radius. People have been struck by the wrapping arm.</li>\n<li>Reload film with the machine <strong>stopped</strong>.</li>\n<li>Watch the <strong>drop zone</strong> where the wrapped bale is set down — keep clear, mind toes.</li>\n<li>Stacking wrapped bales: see Module 06.</li>\n</ul>\n<hr />\n<h2>General machinery good practice</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Read the operator's manual</strong> for each machine — it's the final word.</li>\n<li>Hitch/unhitch on <strong>firm, level ground</strong>; mind crush points between tractor and machine.</li>\n<li>Don't adjust, grease or unblock with it running.</li>\n<li>Keep bystanders out of the working area entirely.</li>\n</ul>\n<hr />\n<h2>Discussion / demonstration</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Point to the PTO guard and anti-rotation chains and explain what they do.</li>\n<li>Recite the blockage-clearing procedure from memory.</li>\n<li>Explain \"stored energy\" and give an example on a baler/mower.</li>\n<li>Show how you'd connect a PTO shaft safely.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Sign-off:</strong> Trainee understands PTO/guarding dangers and can correctly clear a blockage (engine off, parts stopped, tool used).</p>\n<p>Trainee signature: <strong><em>_</em></strong><strong><em>_</em></strong><strong><em>_  Supervisor signature: </em></strong><strong><em>_</em></strong><strong><em>_</em></strong>_  Date: <strong><em>_</em></strong>___</p>"}, {"id": "hay", "group": "Core safety", "short": "Hay & haylage operations", "title": "Module 06 — Hay &amp; Haylage Operations (Field to Stack)", "html": "<h1>Module 06 — Hay &amp; Haylage Operations (Field to Stack)</h1>\n<p><em>Putting it all together: the actual job you'll be doing this summer, and the specific risks at each stage.</em></p>\n<hr />\n<h2>What we're making, and why it changes the risks</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Hay</strong> is grass cut, dried in the field (turned/tedded over several days), then baled dry. Risks: long hot days, dust, fire, machinery, bale handling.</li>\n<li><strong>Haylage</strong> is grass cut, wilted for a shorter time, baled at higher moisture, then <strong>wrapped airtight in plastic film</strong> so it ferments. Extra risks: the <strong>wrapper</strong>, <strong>heavier wetter bales</strong>, and handling/stacking <strong>film-wrapped</strong> bales that can roll.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Both involve the same chain: <strong>mow → ted/turn → row up (rake) → bale → (wrap, for haylage) → cart → stack</strong>. Every stage has a tractor and a PTO machine, and every stage ends with <strong>heavy bales that must be moved and stacked</strong>.</p>\n<hr />\n<h2>Stage-by-stage safety</h2>\n<h3>1. Mowing</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Pre-start checks (Module 03), sharp undamaged blades.</li>\n<li><strong>Walk/check the field</strong> for hazards first: people, animals, ground nesting, debris, hidden ditches, soft ground, slopes.</li>\n<li>Keep bystanders <strong>well clear</strong> — mowers throw stones and blade fragments.</li>\n<li>Mind the slopes (Module 03) — drive up/down, slow, no sharp turns.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>2. Tedding &amp; raking</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Watch folding wings (crush points) and flung debris (eye protection).</li>\n<li>Wait for tines to <strong>stop</strong> before clearing tangled grass.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>3. Baling</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Tall machine, <strong>high centre of gravity</strong> — slow on slopes, avoid holes/drop-offs.</li>\n<li><strong>Never</strong> clear the pick-up or chamber while running (Module 05).</li>\n<li><strong>Stand clear of the rear</strong> when a round bale is ejected.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>4. Wrapping (haylage)</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Stay <strong>outside the rotating arm/ring radius</strong>.</li>\n<li>Reload film with the machine <strong>stopped</strong>.</li>\n<li>Haylage bales are <strong>heavier</strong> (more moisture) — factor that into telehandler load limits.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>5. Carting &amp; stacking — where bales fall on people</h3>\n<p>This stage causes many of the \"struck by / falling object\" deaths.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Move bales with the <strong>right attachment</strong> (bale spike or grab) on the telehandler — <strong>never the loader bucket</strong>, which lets bales roll back toward the cab/operator.</li>\n<li>Carry bales <strong>low</strong>; travel and turn slowly (Module 04).</li>\n<li><strong>No one walks or stands near the telehandler</strong> while it's working or in the stacking area. Falling and rolling bales kill.</li>\n<li>Don't overload trailers; secure loads; <strong>no riding on the load</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<hr />\n<h2>Stacking bales safely</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Build stacks on <strong>firm, level ground</strong>, square and stable, <strong>bonded</strong> (overlapping) like brickwork so they don't topple.</li>\n<li><strong>Don't stack too high</strong> for the base — tall narrow stacks collapse. Follow the farm's agreed maximum.</li>\n<li>Keep stacks <strong>away from overhead power lines</strong>, buildings you don't want to risk, and field edges/slopes.</li>\n<li><strong>Never climb on a bale stack.</strong> People fall from stacks and are crushed by collapsing stacks. If you must reach height, use proper access — not the stack itself.</li>\n<li>When taking a stack down, work from the <strong>top down</strong>, never pull from the bottom (the classic fatal collapse).</li>\n<li>Round bales <strong>roll</strong> — chock them or stack so they can't run away, especially on any slope. A loose round bale can crush someone.</li>\n</ul>\n<hr />\n<h2>Fire &amp; spontaneous heating — a real hay risk</h2>\n<p>Hay baled <strong>too damp</strong> can heat up internally and <strong>spontaneously catch fire</strong> days or weeks later. Dry, dusty conditions plus hot machinery also start field fires.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Bale hay at the <strong>right moisture</strong> — your supervisor will check.</li>\n<li><strong>Monitor stacked hay</strong> for heating in the first weeks: a hot, \"caramel/burning\" smell, steam, or a stack that's hot to a probe is a danger sign — tell your supervisor immediately and <strong>do not</strong> disturb a hot stack (letting air in can cause it to burst into flame).</li>\n<li>Keep a <strong>fire extinguisher on the tractor/baler</strong> and know how to use it.</li>\n<li>Carry a <strong>phone</strong> to call 999. Know the farm's location to give them (Module 09).</li>\n<li>Clear chaff/dust build-up off hot machine parts (manifold, brakes) during long baling days.</li>\n<li>No smoking anywhere near hay, fuel or machinery.</li>\n</ul>\n<hr />\n<h2>Long days, heat and fatigue</h2>\n<p>Haymaking means <strong>long hours racing the weather</strong>. That's exactly when accidents spike.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Drink water regularly; you sweat more than you think in a hot cab.</li>\n<li>Use <strong>sun protection</strong> (hat, sunscreen, cover up) — see Module 07.</li>\n<li>Take <strong>breaks</strong>. Eat. Tiredness slows reactions and judgement.</li>\n<li><strong>\"One more load\" in the dark while exhausted</strong> is when people get hurt. If you're too tired, say so. The weather is not worth your safety.</li>\n</ul>\n<hr />\n<h2>Discussion / demonstration</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Walk through the whole hay/haylage process and name a key hazard at each stage.</li>\n<li>Explain why you use a bale spike/grab and not the bucket.</li>\n<li>Explain how you'd build a safe, stable stack and why you never climb it.</li>\n<li>Describe the signs of a hay stack heating up, and what you'd do.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Sign-off:</strong> Trainee understands the end-to-end hay/haylage job and its specific hazards.</p>\n<p>Trainee signature: <strong><em>_</em></strong><strong><em>_</em></strong><strong><em>_  Supervisor signature: </em></strong><strong><em>_</em></strong><strong><em>_</em></strong>_  Date: <strong><em>_</em></strong>___</p>"}, {"id": "health", "group": "Core safety", "short": "Handling, PPE, noise & dust", "title": "Module 07 — Manual Handling, PPE, Noise, Dust, Vibration &amp; Heat", "html": "<h1>Module 07 — Manual Handling, PPE, Noise, Dust, Vibration &amp; Heat</h1>\n<p><em>The injuries that build up slowly — your back, your hearing, your lungs, your hands — matter just as much as the dramatic ones.</em></p>\n<hr />\n<h2>Manual handling — protect your back</h2>\n<p>Farm work means lifting, carrying and dragging. Your body at 16 is still developing — bad lifting now can cause problems for life.</p>\n<p><strong>Before you lift, think:</strong>\n- <strong>Can a machine do it?</strong> Use the telehandler/loader for anything heavy. Don't lift what a machine should.\n- <strong>Is it too heavy or awkward?</strong> Get help, split the load, or use an aid.\n- <strong>Clear the route</strong> — no trip hazards, know where you're putting it down.</p>\n<p><strong>Good lifting technique:</strong>\n- Feet apart, stable base, close to the load.\n- <strong>Bend your knees, not your back</strong> — keep the back's natural curve.\n- Get a secure grip; lift smoothly with your legs.\n- Keep the load <strong>close to your body</strong>.\n- <strong>Don't twist</strong> while lifting — turn with your feet.\n- Put it down the same way — knees, not back.</p>\n<p><strong>On the farm specifically:</strong> twine bales, fence posts, feed bags, drums, machine parts. If you're straining, stop — that's the warning, not a challenge.</p>\n<hr />\n<h2>PPE — what to wear and when</h2>\n<p>PPE is the <strong>last line of defence</strong>, but it matters. Your supervisor provides it; you wear it.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>PPE</th>\n<th>When</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Safety boots</strong> (steel toe, good grip)</td>\n<td><strong>Always</strong>, every day</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Ear protection</strong> (plugs or muffs)</td>\n<td>Noisy machines, long cab time, anything where you'd have to raise your voice</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Dust mask</strong> (FFP2/FFP3)</td>\n<td>Mouldy/dusty hay, dusty cab work, sweeping</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Eye protection</strong></td>\n<td>Tedding/mowing (flung debris), grinding, anything that could flick into your eyes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Gloves</strong></td>\n<td>Handling twine/wire, rough materials, chemicals (correct type)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Hi-vis</strong></td>\n<td>Around moving vehicles, road work, poor light</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Sun protection</strong></td>\n<td>Long days in the open (see below)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Hard hat</strong></td>\n<td>Where there's a risk of falling objects</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Loose clothing is a hazard around machinery — keep it <strong>fitted and tucked in</strong> (Module 05).</p>\n<hr />\n<h2>Noise — you only get one set of ears</h2>\n<p>Tractors, balers and PTO machines are loud, and <strong>hearing loss is permanent and painless</strong> as it happens — you won't notice until it's gone.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>If you have to <strong>raise your voice</strong> to be heard at arm's length, it's loud enough to need ear protection.</li>\n<li>Wear plugs or muffs and <strong>keep them in</strong> — taking them out \"just for a minute\" repeatedly defeats them.</li>\n<li>Keep cab doors/windows closed where practical.</li>\n</ul>\n<hr />\n<h2>Dust &amp; mould — protect your lungs</h2>\n<p>Hay and haylage are dusty, and mouldy hay carries spores that cause <strong>\"Farmer's Lung\"</strong> and other serious, sometimes permanent, lung disease.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Wear an <strong>FFP3 dust mask</strong> when handling dusty or mouldy hay, or working in dust.</li>\n<li>Work <strong>upwind</strong> of dust where you can; keep cab filters in good order and windows shut in heavy dust.</li>\n<li>Don't disturb visibly mouldy bales without a mask.</li>\n</ul>\n<hr />\n<h2>Vibration — HAVS and whole-body</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Hand-Arm Vibration (HAVS):</strong> from vibrating tools — causes painful, permanent nerve and circulation damage (\"white finger\"). Limit time on vibrating tools; keep hands warm; report tingling/numbness early.</li>\n<li><strong>Whole-body vibration:</strong> from bouncing around in the cab on rough ground all day causes back problems. Keep the <strong>seat suspension set for your weight</strong>, drive at sensible speed for the ground, and take breaks.</li>\n</ul>\n<hr />\n<h2>Heat, sun &amp; dehydration</h2>\n<p>Summer haymaking = long hours in the sun and hot cabs.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Drink water often</strong> — don't wait until you're thirsty. Keep a bottle in the cab.</li>\n<li>Cover up, wear a hat, use <strong>sunscreen</strong> on exposed skin (skin cancer is a real long-term farm risk).</li>\n<li>Recognise <strong>heat exhaustion</strong>: headache, dizziness, feeling sick, cramps, heavy sweating then clammy/pale — get into shade, cool down, drink, tell someone. Heatstroke (confusion, hot dry skin, collapse) is a <strong>999 emergency</strong>.</li>\n<li>Take breaks in the shade.</li>\n</ul>\n<hr />\n<h2>Discussion / demonstration</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Demonstrate a correct lift (knees not back, load close, no twist).</li>\n<li>Say when you'd put on ear protection, a dust mask, and eye protection.</li>\n<li>Explain what Farmer's Lung is and how you avoid it.</li>\n<li>Describe the early signs of heat exhaustion.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Sign-off:</strong> Trainee can lift safely and knows what PPE to use when, and the health risks PPE protects against.</p>\n<p>Trainee signature: <strong><em>_</em></strong><strong><em>_</em></strong><strong><em>_  Supervisor signature: </em></strong><strong><em>_</em></strong><strong><em>_</em></strong>_  Date: <strong><em>_</em></strong>___</p>"}, {"id": "site", "group": "Core safety", "short": "Site hazards", "title": "Module 08 — Site Hazards (Height, Electricity, Fire, Slurry, Livestock, Chemicals)", "html": "<h1>Module 08 — Site Hazards (Height, Electricity, Fire, Slurry, Livestock, Chemicals)</h1>\n<p><em>The wider farm hazards beyond the machine you're driving. Even if you're \"just\" haymaking, you share the site with all of these.</em></p>\n<hr />\n<h2>Working at height</h2>\n<p>Falls — from machines, trailers, stacks, ladders and fragile roofs — are a major cause of farm deaths.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Avoid working at height</strong> where you can. Don't climb on bale stacks, trailers or machines to reach things.</li>\n<li>Use a <strong>proper ladder or platform</strong>, secured, on firm ground, three points of contact.</li>\n<li><strong>Fragile roofs (asbestos cement, fibre-cement, rooflights) kill</strong> — people fall straight through. <strong>Never walk on a barn roof.</strong> It's a job for specialists with proper equipment.</li>\n<li>Never use telehandler forks as a platform or to lift a person except in a <strong>purpose-made, tested cage</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<hr />\n<h2>Electricity &amp; overhead power lines</h2>\n<p>A telehandler boom, tipping trailer, or a high load can hit overhead lines — and <strong>electricity can arc across a gap without contact</strong>. This is one of the most preventable farm deaths.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Map every overhead line on the farm</strong> and know the safe working clearances. Mark them at the induction (Module 01).</li>\n<li>Plan stacking, tipping and high-reach work <strong>well away</strong> from lines.</li>\n<li>Watch for <strong>underground cables</strong> when digging.</li>\n<li><strong>If a machine contacts a line:</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Stay in the cab</strong> — you're safest there.</li>\n<li>If you can, <strong>drive clear</strong> of the line.</li>\n<li><strong>Warn everyone to keep back</strong> and not touch the machine.</li>\n<li>Call the emergency number on the nearest pole / 105 (UK power network) and 999.</li>\n<li>Only if there's fire and you <em>must</em> get out: <strong>jump clear with feet together, don't touch machine and ground at once</strong>, then <strong>shuffle/bunny-hop</strong> away with small steps (the ground itself is live in a gradient).</li>\n<li>Damaged fallen lines: assume <strong>live</strong>, keep everyone <strong>well back</strong>, call for help.</li>\n</ul>\n<hr />\n<h2>Fire</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Know where <strong>extinguishers</strong> are (buildings and machines) and how to use them (pull pin, aim at base, sweep).</li>\n<li>Biggest farm fire risks: machinery (hot parts + dust/chaff), fuel, <strong>hay spontaneously heating</strong> (Module 06), and electrical faults.</li>\n<li>Keep dust/chaff cleared off hot machine parts on long days.</li>\n<li>No smoking near fuel, hay or machinery.</li>\n<li>If fire breaks out: get clear, raise the alarm, call <strong>999</strong>, only fight a small fire if safe and you have an escape route behind you. <strong>People before property.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<hr />\n<h2>Slurry, silage effluent &amp; confined spaces</h2>\n<p>Even on a hay/haylage farm there may be slurry stores, tanks, pits or grain stores.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Slurry gas</strong> (especially hydrogen sulphide, released when slurry is mixed/agitated) can <strong>kill in seconds</strong> and has killed whole families. <strong>Stay away from slurry stores during mixing</strong>; keep clear of the area.</li>\n<li><strong>Confined spaces</strong> (tanks, pits, silos, augers, grain bins) can have <strong>no oxygen or toxic gas</strong>, and grain/material can <strong>engulf and suffocate</strong>. <strong>Never enter a confined space</strong> — it is a specialist job with permits, gas testing and rescue plans. This is <strong>not</strong> a job for a young, inexperienced worker.</li>\n</ul>\n<hr />\n<h2>Livestock</h2>\n<p>Cattle are a leading cause of farm deaths — they crush, trample and charge, especially <strong>cows with calves</strong> and <strong>bulls</strong>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Don't enter fields/pens with cattle unless told to, and never alone if you're inexperienced.</li>\n<li>Keep an <strong>escape route</strong>; stay near the edge, never get cornered.</li>\n<li>Move calmly and quietly; never get between a cow and her calf.</li>\n<li>Treat <strong>every bull as dangerous</strong>.</li>\n<li>Watch out when crossing fields with stock during haymaking — close gates, keep stock and machinery apart.</li>\n</ul>\n<hr />\n<h2>Chemicals &amp; fuel</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Pesticides</strong> require training and certification (16+ with a recognised certificate). Don't handle or apply them without it.</li>\n<li>Diesel, oils, AdBlue, two-stroke: store correctly, avoid skin contact, wash hands before eating, no eating/drinking around chemicals.</li>\n<li>Read labels and <strong>safety data sheets</strong>; wear the PPE the label specifies.</li>\n<li>Know where the <strong>eye wash / first aid</strong> is for splashes (Module 09).</li>\n</ul>\n<hr />\n<h2>Discussion / demonstration</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Point out (or mark on a map) every overhead power line on the farm.</li>\n<li>Say exactly what you'd do if the telehandler touched a power line.</li>\n<li>Explain why you must never walk on an old barn roof.</li>\n<li>Explain why you never enter a slurry tank or grain store.</li>\n<li>Describe how to behave around cattle.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Sign-off:</strong> Trainee understands the wider site hazards and the correct responses.</p>\n<p>Trainee signature: <strong><em>_</em></strong><strong><em>_</em></strong><strong><em>_  Supervisor signature: </em></strong><strong><em>_</em></strong><strong><em>_</em></strong>_  Date: <strong><em>_</em></strong>___</p>"}, {"id": "emergencies", "group": "Core safety", "short": "Emergencies & first aid", "title": "Module 09 — Emergencies, First Aid &amp; Reporting", "html": "<h1>Module 09 — Emergencies, First Aid &amp; Reporting</h1>\n<p><em>When something goes wrong, the first few minutes matter most. Know this before you need it.</em></p>\n<hr />\n<h2>In an emergency — the order of actions</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Stop the danger if you safely can.</strong> Safe Stop any machine. Don't rush into danger and become a second casualty — that's how rescuers get killed (slurry gas, electricity, machinery).</li>\n<li><strong>Make the area safe</strong> — switch off power/PTO, keep others back.</li>\n<li><strong>Check the casualty</strong> — are they responsive? Breathing?</li>\n<li><strong>Call 999</strong> (or 112). Ask for <strong>Ambulance</strong> (and Fire/Police if needed).</li>\n<li><strong>Give first aid</strong> within your training. Don't move someone with a possible spinal injury unless they're in immediate danger.</li>\n<li><strong>Send someone to meet the ambulance</strong> at the road/gate to guide them in — farms are hard to find.</li>\n</ol>\n<hr />\n<h2>Calling 999 from the farm — have this ready NOW</h2>\n<p>Fill this in during induction and keep it in the cab / on your phone:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th></th>\n<th></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Farm name</strong></td>\n<td>Truleigh Manor Farm</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Full address &amp; postcode</strong></td>\n<td>Edburton Road, Henfield, <strong>BN5 9LL</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>what3words — farm entrance</strong></td>\n<td><code>///chicken.airless.hazy</code> (https://w3w.co/chicken.airless.hazy)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>what3words — Truleigh Sands</strong></td>\n<td><code>///playback.poker.touched</code> (https://w3w.co/playback.poker.touched)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Field name/number</strong></td>\n<td>Give the field you're in (see <a href=\"#/read/map\">Module 16</a>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Nearest road / gate to direct ambulance to</strong></td>\n<td>Farm entrance off Edburton Road (<code>///chicken.airless.hazy</code>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Nearest defibrillator (if any)</strong></td>\n<td><strong><em>_</em></strong><strong><em>_</em></strong><strong><em>_</em></strong><strong><em>_</em></strong><strong><em>_</em></strong>______</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Emergency contacts</strong></td>\n<td>Ben Hogan <strong>07876 683949</strong> · Sandra Windus <strong>07901 521832</strong> · Office <strong>01903 816065</strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><em>See the printable <a href=\"#/read/emergency-card\">Emergency Information Card</a> — keep one in every cab.</em></p>\n<p>When you call: stay calm, say <strong>where</strong> (postcode + what3words), <strong>what happened</strong>, <strong>how many</strong> hurt, and <strong>what injuries</strong>. Don't hang up until told to. If you can, send someone to the gate to flag the ambulance down.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Mobile signal can be poor in fields.</strong> Know the spots with signal. In a real emergency, <strong>112</strong> works on any network and can use any mast. A 999 text service exists if you've pre-registered.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<hr />\n<h2>Common farm injuries — quick first aid pointers</h2>\n<p><em>(These are pointers only — a proper first aid course is strongly recommended. The farm should have a trained first aider and a stocked kit.)</em></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Severe bleeding:</strong> press hard directly on the wound with a clean pad, keep pressing, raise the limb if you can, call 999. Don't remove a soaked pad — add more on top.</li>\n<li><strong>Crush / entanglement / amputation:</strong> call 999 immediately. Control bleeding. <strong>Keep any severed part</strong> — wrap in clean material, put in a bag, keep cool (not directly on ice) and send with the casualty.</li>\n<li><strong>Broken bones:</strong> don't move unnecessarily; support the limb; 999.</li>\n<li><strong>Unconscious but breathing:</strong> put in the <strong>recovery position</strong>, monitor breathing, 999.</li>\n<li><strong>Not breathing:</strong> start <strong>CPR</strong> if trained; 999 will talk you through it; use a defibrillator if available.</li>\n<li><strong>Chemical in eyes/skin:</strong> flush with lots of clean water for at least 10–15 minutes; take the product label/safety data sheet to hospital.</li>\n<li><strong>Suspected slurry gas / electric shock:</strong> <strong>do not approach</strong> until the area is confirmed safe — call 999 and keep clear.</li>\n<li><strong>Heatstroke:</strong> move to shade, cool rapidly, 999 if confused/collapsed.</li>\n</ul>\n<hr />\n<h2>First aid kit &amp; welfare</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Know where the <strong>first aid kit</strong> and <strong>eye wash</strong> are (yard and, ideally, in the tractor/telehandler).</li>\n<li>Tell your supervisor if anything is used up so it can be restocked.</li>\n<li>Clean water, soap and a place to rest should be available.</li>\n</ul>\n<hr />\n<h2>Reporting — accidents AND near misses</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Report every accident, injury and near miss</strong> to your supervisor straight away — even if no one was hurt. A near miss is a free warning; reporting it stops the next one being a tragedy.</li>\n<li>The farm should record incidents in an <strong>accident book</strong>.</li>\n<li>Some incidents must be reported to HSE by the <strong>employer</strong> under <strong>RIDDOR</strong> (Reporting of Injuries, Diseases and Dangerous Occurrences Regulations) — e.g. deaths, specified serious injuries, injuries causing more than 7 days off work, and certain dangerous occurrences. <strong>That's the employer's duty — your job is to tell your supervisor everything.</strong></li>\n<li>Never hide a mistake or a near miss out of embarrassment. Speaking up is exactly what a good worker does.</li>\n</ul>\n<hr />\n<h2>Discussion / demonstration</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Say the full 999 information for this farm from the card above, out loud.</li>\n<li>Show the recovery position (on a willing volunteer or describe it).</li>\n<li>Explain what you'd do if you found someone caught in a baler.</li>\n<li>Explain why you don't rush straight in to a slurry/electrical incident.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Sign-off:</strong> Trainee knows the emergency procedure, can call for help with farm location, and will report all incidents and near misses.</p>\n<p>Trainee signature: <strong><em>_</em></strong><strong><em>_</em></strong><strong><em>_  Supervisor signature: </em></strong><strong><em>_</em></strong><strong><em>_</em></strong>_  Date: <strong><em>_</em></strong>___</p>"}, {"id": "hb-index", "group": "Machinery handbook", "short": "Handbook overview", "title": "Module 15 — Machinery Handbook", "html": "<h1>Module 15 — Machinery Handbook</h1>\n<p><strong>Truleigh Manor Farm — safe operation &amp; maintenance of our kit</strong></p>\n<p>This is the machine-by-machine reference for the equipment used in our hay and haylage operation.\nIt builds on the general machine modules — read those <strong>first</strong>, because the rules in them apply to\neverything here:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Module 02 — Safe Stop</strong> (handbrake → neutral → engine off → key out)</li>\n<li><strong>Module 03 — Tractors</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Module 04 — Telehandlers</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Module 05 — PTO &amp; field machinery</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<hr />\n<h2>Three rules that apply to every machine in this handbook</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>The operator's manual is the final word.</strong> This handbook gives the safe routine and the key\n   numbers; the manual in the cab gives the exact figures, intervals and warnings for that machine.\n   If they ever disagree, follow the manual and tell your supervisor.</li>\n<li><strong>Only operate a machine you have been trained and signed off on</strong> (Module 13). Being shown once\n   is not being signed off.</li>\n<li><strong>Safe Stop before you leave the seat, before anyone approaches, and before you touch anything</strong>\n   — for checks, fuelling, greasing, clearing a blockage, or adjusting.</li>\n</ol>\n<hr />\n<h2>How to use this handbook</h2>\n<p>For each machine: read the page → have it demonstrated → operate it under supervision → get the\nsign-off line filled in. Do the <strong>daily checks</strong>, <strong>fuelling</strong> and <strong>greasing</strong> as part of the\nroutine, not as an afterthought.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Page</th>\n<th>Machine</th>\n<th>Type</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><a href=\"#/read/hb-fuel\">A1 — Fuelling up</a></td>\n<td><strong>Shared procedure</strong> — diesel &amp; AdBlue</td>\n<td>All</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><a href=\"#/read/hb-grease\">A2 — Greasing &amp; daily maintenance</a></td>\n<td><strong>Shared procedure</strong></td>\n<td>All</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><a href=\"#/read/hb-puma\">B1 — Case IH Puma 240 CVX</a></td>\n<td>Tractor (~240 hp, CVT)</td>\n<td>Tractor</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><a href=\"#/read/hb-jd\">B2 — John Deere 6150R</a></td>\n<td>Tractor (~150 hp)</td>\n<td>Tractor</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><a href=\"#/read/hb-541\">C1 — JCB 541-70 Loadall</a></td>\n<td>Telehandler (4.1 t / 7.0 m)</td>\n<td>Telehandler</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><a href=\"#/read/hb-516\">C2 — JCB 516-40</a></td>\n<td>Compact telehandler (1.6 t / 4.0 m)</td>\n<td>Telehandler</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><a href=\"#/read/hb-tedder\">D1 — Kuhn GF 8501</a></td>\n<td>Rotary tedder (~8.5 m)</td>\n<td>Field machine</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><a href=\"#/read/hb-rake\">D2 — Kuhn GA twin rotor rake</a></td>\n<td>Twin-rotor rake</td>\n<td>Field machine</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><a href=\"#/read/hb-baler\">D3 — New Holland BB940A</a></td>\n<td>Large square baler</td>\n<td>Field machine</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><a href=\"#/read/hb-mowers\">D4 — Kuhn front &amp; rear 3 m mowers</a></td>\n<td>Disc mower combination (~6 m)</td>\n<td>Field machine</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><a href=\"#/read/hb-grabs\">E1 — Bale grabs &amp; squeeze</a></td>\n<td>Telehandler attachments</td>\n<td>Attachment</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><a href=\"#/read/hb-trailers\">E2 — Bale trailers</a></td>\n<td>Trailed transport</td>\n<td>Trailer</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<hr />\n<h2>What this handbook does NOT cover</h2>\n<p>This is an <strong>operator's safety handbook</strong>, not a workshop service manual. It deliberately does not\ngive engine internals, exact torque values, fluid grades or full service schedules — those come from\neach machine's manufacturer manual and are the job of an experienced mechanic or the dealer. Your\nmaintenance role as a young operator is <strong>daily checks, fuelling and greasing</strong> done safely.</p>"}, {"id": "hb-fuel", "group": "Machinery handbook", "short": "Fuelling up", "title": "A1 — Fuelling Up (Diesel &amp; AdBlue)", "html": "<h1>A1 — Fuelling Up (Diesel &amp; AdBlue)</h1>\n<p><em>Shared procedure for all tractors and telehandlers. Refuelling is routine — which is exactly why\npeople get careless. Diesel is a fire and environmental hazard, and a slip/trip hazard around the\nfuel store.</em></p>\n<hr />\n<h2>Before you fuel</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Engine OFF. Safe Stop</strong> (handbrake, neutral, key out). Never refuel a running engine.</li>\n<li><strong>No smoking, no naked flames, no phones</strong> at the fuel store or pump.</li>\n<li>Park on <strong>firm, level ground</strong> by the fuel store.</li>\n<li>Know where the <strong>spill kit</strong> and <strong>fire extinguisher</strong> are at the fuel store.</li>\n<li>Wear <strong>gloves</strong>; avoid breathing fumes; don't get diesel on your skin or clothes.</li>\n</ul>\n<hr />\n<h2>Diesel — the routine</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Open the fuel store / bowser as instructed; check the <strong>nozzle and hose</strong> are sound.</li>\n<li>Wipe around the filler cap so dirt doesn't drop into the tank; remove the cap.</li>\n<li>Put the nozzle <strong>fully into the filler</strong> and keep hold of it the whole time — <strong>don't walk away</strong>.</li>\n<li>Fill <strong>steadily</strong>; listen and watch for the level. <strong>Stop before it overflows</strong> — don't \"top off\"\n   to the brim, especially on a hot day (diesel expands and will overflow).</li>\n<li>Replace the cap firmly. Wipe up any drips with the spill kit/rag.</li>\n<li>Log the fuel taken if the farm keeps a fuel book.</li>\n</ol>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Static electricity</strong> can ignite fuel vapour. Keep the nozzle in contact with the filler neck\nwhile filling. Never fill a container on the back of a plastic-lined truck bed.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<hr />\n<h2>AdBlue / DEF (Puma 240 CVX, 6150R, modern telehandlers)</h2>\n<p>Modern diesel engines use <strong>AdBlue</strong> (a urea solution, also called DEF) in a <strong>separate blue-capped\ntank</strong> to clean the exhaust. It is <strong>not fuel</strong> and must never go in the diesel tank — and diesel\nmust never go in the AdBlue tank. Getting them mixed up causes expensive engine/exhaust damage.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Use the <strong>blue AdBlue filler</strong> only, with the correct nozzle.</li>\n<li>AdBlue is mildly corrosive and crystallises — <strong>wipe up spills with water</strong> and rinse, don't leave\n  white deposits on paint/connectors.</li>\n<li>Keep it clean — dirt in AdBlue blocks the system. Don't decant it through a dirty funnel.</li>\n<li>Wash hands after handling.</li>\n</ul>\n<hr />\n<h2>Spills &amp; the environment</h2>\n<p>A diesel spill that reaches a ditch, drain or watercourse is a <strong>pollution offence</strong> and can kill\nfish and wildlife — and Truleigh Manor has brooks, lakes and water features (see the field map).</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Contain any spill immediately</strong> with the spill kit (granules/socks). Don't hose it into a drain.</li>\n<li>Tell your supervisor about <strong>every</strong> spill, however small.</li>\n<li>Never refuel right next to a ditch, pond or drain if it can be avoided.</li>\n</ul>\n<hr />\n<h2>Fuel store good practice</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Keep the store <strong>locked</strong> and the area free of trip hazards and clutter.</li>\n<li>Report low fuel, leaks, or a damaged hose/nozzle.</li>\n<li>Don't fill jerry cans unless shown how, and only in <strong>approved metal/plastic fuel containers</strong>,\n  on the ground, never in a vehicle.</li>\n</ul>\n<hr />\n<h2>Quick do / don't</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>✅ Do</th>\n<th>❌ Don't</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Engine off, Safe Stop, before fuelling</td>\n<td>Refuel a running or hot-restart engine</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Hold the nozzle the whole time</td>\n<td>Walk away mid-fill</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Stop before the brim</td>\n<td>Overfill / top off on a hot day</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Use the blue filler for AdBlue only</td>\n<td>Mix diesel and AdBlue</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Contain spills, tell supervisor</td>\n<td>Hose fuel toward a ditch or drain</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Sign-off:</strong> Trainee can refuel diesel and top up AdBlue safely, and deal with a spill.</p>\n<p>Trainee: <strong><em>_</em></strong><strong><em>_</em></strong><strong><em>_  Supervisor: </em></strong><strong><em>_</em></strong><strong><em>_</em></strong>_  Date: <strong><em>_</em></strong>___</p>"}, {"id": "hb-grease", "group": "Machinery handbook", "short": "Greasing & daily checks", "title": "A2 — Greasing &amp; Daily Operator Maintenance", "html": "<h1>A2 — Greasing &amp; Daily Operator Maintenance</h1>\n<p><em>Shared procedure for all machines. Greasing and daily checks are the maintenance jobs a young\noperator does — and most maintenance deaths happen because a machine was running, or moved, while\nsomeone worked on it. Get the safety routine right every time.</em></p>\n<hr />\n<h2>The golden rule of any maintenance: SAFE STOP first</h2>\n<p>Before you grease, check, or touch any machine — even for \"just a second\":</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Handbrake on</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Controls in neutral; lower all attachments</strong> (loader, forks, linkage, mower beds) <strong>to the ground</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Engine off</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Key out and in your pocket</strong> (so no one can start it while you're at the sharp end)</li>\n<li><strong>Wait</strong> for any rotating part to <strong>completely stop</strong></li>\n</ol>\n<p>If you must work under something raised (rare — avoid it), it <strong>must be physically propped or locked</strong>\n— never trust hydraulics to hold it up.</p>\n<hr />\n<h2>Greasing — how and where</h2>\n<p>Grease keeps pins, pivots and bearings from seizing and wearing out. A grease gun pushes grease\nthrough a <strong>nipple</strong> into the joint.</p>\n<p><strong>How to use a grease gun:</strong>\n- Wipe the <strong>grease nipple</strong> clean first so you don't push grit in.\n- Push the coupler squarely onto the nipple; pump until you feel/see fresh grease reach the joint\n  (a little squeezing out is normal — wipe the excess).\n- If a nipple won't take grease, <strong>don't force it</strong> — tell your supervisor (it may be blocked or the\n  joint damaged).\n- Use the <strong>correct grease</strong>; don't mix random types.</p>\n<p><strong>Typical points to grease (check each machine's page and manual for the exact list):</strong></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Area</th>\n<th>Examples</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>PTO shafts</strong></td>\n<td>Universal joints at each end, the sliding inner tube, and the guard bearings — these are high-wear and often missed</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Three-point linkage / drawbar</strong></td>\n<td>Pins, top-link, lift arms, pickup hitch</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Loader / telehandler</strong></td>\n<td>Boom pivot pins, ram pin ends, headstock/quick-hitch, axle pivots</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Tedder / rake</strong></td>\n<td>Rotor gearbox arms, wheel pivots, folding rams' pins</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Mower</strong></td>\n<td>Cutterbar (per manual), pivot points, breakback pivot</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Baler</strong></td>\n<td>Many points — pickup, plunger area, knotter (per manual/auto-lube), bale chamber rollers</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Trailer</strong></td>\n<td>Hitch eye, brake linkages, tipping ram pins, axle points</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Many balers and bigger machines have <strong>auto-lube</strong> or grease banks (a row of nipples on one block)\n— your supervisor will show you the bank and the schedule.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<hr />\n<h2>Daily checks (do before first use each day)</h2>\n<p>A quick walk-round catches most faults before they bite:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>☐ <strong>Oil</strong> — engine oil on the dipstick within marks (engine cold/level ground).</li>\n<li>☐ <strong>Coolant</strong> — level in the expansion tank (only check when <strong>cold</strong> — never open a hot cap).</li>\n<li>☐ <strong>Hydraulic oil</strong> — sight glass/dipstick level.</li>\n<li>☐ <strong>Diesel &amp; AdBlue</strong> — enough for the job (see A1).</li>\n<li>☐ <strong>Tyres</strong> — condition, no big cuts, pressures look right, wheel nuts present.</li>\n<li>☐ <strong>Leaks</strong> — look under the machine for oil/coolant/hydraulic/fuel pooling.</li>\n<li>☐ <strong>Guards</strong> — all present and secure, especially <strong>PTO guards and chains</strong>.</li>\n<li>☐ <strong>Lights, beacon, indicators, brake lights, mirrors, horn</strong> — clean and working.</li>\n<li>☐ <strong>Steps, handholds, glass</strong> — clean of mud (slip prevention / visibility).</li>\n<li>☐ <strong>Attachment / implement</strong> — pins, clips and quick-hitch locked.</li>\n</ul>\n<hr />\n<h2>After maintenance — before you restart</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Replace every guard</strong> you removed.</li>\n<li>Remove any tools, rags or grease guns from the machine.</li>\n<li>Make sure <strong>no one is near</strong> moving parts.</li>\n<li>Then, and only then, start up.</li>\n</ul>\n<hr />\n<h2>Report defects — don't just live with them</h2>\n<p>If something is worn, leaking, loose, missing a guard, or \"not quite right\":</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Take the machine out of use</strong> if it's a safety item (brakes, guards, steering, hitch).</li>\n<li><strong>Tell your supervisor</strong> and, if the farm has one, write it in the <strong>defect/maintenance log</strong>.</li>\n<li>Never \"make do\" with a broken guard or a dodgy brake to get the job finished.</li>\n</ul>\n<hr />\n<h2>Quick do / don't</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>✅ Do</th>\n<th>❌ Don't</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Safe Stop + key in pocket before greasing</td>\n<td>Grease/adjust with the engine running</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Wait for all parts to stop</td>\n<td>Reach into a coasting machine</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Wipe nipples, use correct grease</td>\n<td>Force grease into a blocked nipple</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Prop/lock anything raised you work under</td>\n<td>Trust hydraulics to hold a load up</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Replace guards before restarting</td>\n<td>Run a machine with a guard off</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Report and log defects</td>\n<td>\"Make do\" with a known fault</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Sign-off:</strong> Trainee can carry out daily checks and grease a machine safely (Safe Stop, correct points, guards replaced).</p>\n<p>Trainee: <strong><em>_</em></strong><strong><em>_</em></strong><strong><em>_  Supervisor: </em></strong><strong><em>_</em></strong><strong><em>_</em></strong>_  Date: <strong><em>_</em></strong>___</p>"}, {"id": "hb-puma", "group": "Machinery handbook", "short": "Case IH Puma 240 CVX", "title": "B1 — Case IH Puma 240 CVX (Tractor)", "html": "<h1>B1 — Case IH Puma 240 CVX (Tractor)</h1>\n<p><em>Read <a href=\"#/read/tractors\">Module 03 — Tractors</a> first. This page adds the points specific to the\nPuma 240 CVX, our biggest tractor.</em></p>\n<hr />\n<h2>At a glance</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th></th>\n<th></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Type</td>\n<td>High-horsepower tractor, ~240 hp</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Transmission</td>\n<td><strong>CVX = continuously variable transmission (CVT)</strong> — no gears to change; speed is stepless via the throttle/drive lever</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Fuel</td>\n<td>Diesel <strong>+ AdBlue/DEF</strong> (blue cap) — see <a href=\"#/read/hb-fuel\">A1</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Why extra care</td>\n<td>It's heavy, powerful and wide — big PTO machines (baler, mowers), heavy draft, and more momentum than the 6150R</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<blockquote>\n<p>The CVX has no clutch pedal to \"stall\" it — it can creep or surge if you're heavy on the drive\ncontrol. Learn the controls <strong>stationary, in a safe space</strong>, before working with it.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<hr />\n<h2>Pre-start daily checks</h2>\n<p>Do the <a href=\"#/read/hb-grease\">A2 daily checks</a>, plus on the Puma:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>☐ Diesel <strong>and AdBlue</strong> levels.</li>\n<li>☐ CVX transmission / hydraulic oil level (sight glass).</li>\n<li>☐ Front linkage/PTO (if fitted, for the front mower) — pins, guard, hoses.</li>\n<li>☐ Cab clean, <strong>seatbelt</strong> working, fire extinguisher present.</li>\n<li>☐ Wheel weights / ballast and tyre pressures suit the job (heavy draft work).</li>\n</ul>\n<hr />\n<h2>Safe operation</h2>\n<p><strong>Start-up</strong>\n- Get in using <strong>three points of contact</strong>; settle the <strong>seat suspension</strong> to your weight.\n- <strong>Seatbelt on.</strong> Start only from the seat, drive lever in neutral/park, PTO off.\n- Let it warm up; watch the dash for warnings (AdBlue/DEF, hydraulics, engine).</p>\n<p><strong>Driving the CVT</strong>\n- Move off <strong>gently</strong> — the CVX responds smoothly but instantly; no gear changes, so small inputs\n  make big differences at first.\n- Set a <strong>speed limit / cruise</strong> appropriate to the yard, field or road and stick to it.\n- Use the <strong>handbrake/park</strong> every time you stop — don't rely on the transmission holding it.</p>\n<p><strong>In work (PTO machines)</strong>\n- Match <strong>PTO speed</strong> (540/1000) to the implement — wrong speed damages kit and is dangerous.\n- Engage PTO at <strong>low revs</strong>, build up gently. Never engage at full throttle.\n- Keep everyone clear of the PTO shaft and implement (Module 05).</p>\n<p><strong>Transport / road</strong>\n- Latch the <strong>brake pedals together</strong> for the road.\n- Mind the <strong>width and length</strong> with mounted/towed kit, especially in our gateways (see field map).\n- Beacon on; obey the tractor-on-road rules in Module 03 (licence, width limits at 16).</p>\n<p><strong>Slopes</strong>\n- Heavy + high loader/load = higher tipping risk. Up/down not across, slow, no sharp turns\n  (Module 03). Take extra care on Sands Hill, The Hill and banky ground.</p>\n<hr />\n<h2>Fuelling &amp; AdBlue</h2>\n<p>Engine off, Safe Stop, no smoking. Diesel in the standard filler; <strong>AdBlue only in the blue filler</strong>.\nDon't run the AdBlue tank dry — the engine may de-rate. See <a href=\"#/read/hb-fuel\">A1</a>.</p>\n<h2>Greasing &amp; operator maintenance</h2>\n<p>Safe Stop, key in pocket. Grease the <strong>front and rear linkage pins, top link, pickup hitch, loader\npins (if loader fitted), and both ends + sliding tube of any PTO shaft</strong> in use. Check oil, coolant\n(cold only), hydraulic level, tyres and that all guards are on. See <a href=\"#/read/hb-grease\">A2</a>.</p>\n<h2>Shutdown / parking</h2>\n<p>Firm level ground, lower linkage/loader to the ground, drive lever to neutral/park, <strong>handbrake on,\nengine off, key out.</strong> Don't leave it running unattended.</p>\n<hr />\n<h2>Quick do / don't</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>✅ Do</th>\n<th>❌ Don't</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Seatbelt on, learn CVT controls stationary first</td>\n<td>Move off sharply / surge in the yard</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Handbrake every stop</td>\n<td>Rely on the transmission to hold on a slope</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Match &amp; gently engage PTO speed</td>\n<td>Slam the PTO in at full revs</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>AdBlue in blue filler only</td>\n<td>Put AdBlue in diesel tank (or vice-versa)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Up/down slopes, slow</td>\n<td>Turn across a slope with a high load</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Sign-off:</strong> Trainee is competent to operate the Case IH Puma 240 CVX under the supervision level set in Module 13.</p>\n<p>Trainee: <strong><em>_</em></strong><strong><em>_</em></strong><strong><em>_  Supervisor: </em></strong><strong><em>_</em></strong><strong><em>_</em></strong>_  Date: <strong><em>_</em></strong>___</p>"}, {"id": "hb-jd", "group": "Machinery handbook", "short": "John Deere 6150R", "title": "B2 — John Deere 6150R (Tractor)", "html": "<h1>B2 — John Deere 6150R (Tractor)</h1>\n<p><em>Read <a href=\"#/read/tractors\">Module 03 — Tractors</a> first. This page adds the points specific to the\nJohn Deere 6150R.</em></p>\n<hr />\n<h2>At a glance</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th></th>\n<th></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Type</td>\n<td>Mid-large tractor, ~150 hp</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Transmission</td>\n<td>Powershift / AutoQuad-type (depending on spec) — know whether yours has a clutch pedal and how the ranges work</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Fuel</td>\n<td>Diesel <strong>+ AdBlue/DEF</strong> (blue cap) — see <a href=\"#/read/hb-fuel\">A1</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Typical jobs here</td>\n<td>Tedding, raking, front + rear mowing, carting bales, general work</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Confirm your exact transmission type and control layout with your supervisor — Deere offers\nseveral. Learn <strong>where every control is before you move</strong>.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<hr />\n<h2>Pre-start daily checks</h2>\n<p>Do the <a href=\"#/read/hb-grease\">A2 daily checks</a>, plus on the 6150R:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>☐ Diesel <strong>and AdBlue</strong> levels.</li>\n<li>☐ Transmission/hydraulic oil level.</li>\n<li>☐ Front linkage &amp; front PTO (for the front mower) — pins, guard, hydraulics.</li>\n<li>☐ <strong>Seatbelt</strong> and <strong>ROPS/cab</strong> sound; fire extinguisher present.</li>\n<li>☐ Tyres and any ballast/wheel weights suited to the implement.</li>\n</ul>\n<hr />\n<h2>Safe operation</h2>\n<p><strong>Start-up</strong>\n- Three points of contact in; set seat suspension; <strong>seatbelt on</strong>.\n- Start from the seat only, transmission in neutral/park, PTO off.\n- Watch the display for warnings (AdBlue/DEF, DPF/regen, hydraulics).</p>\n<p><strong>Driving</strong>\n- Move off smoothly; select range/speed appropriate to the task.\n- <strong>Handbrake/park at every stop.</strong>\n- Know how the <strong>hitch/SCV (spool) controls</strong> work before connecting implements and hydraulics.</p>\n<p><strong>In work (PTO machines — tedder, rake, mower, baler)</strong>\n- Set the correct <strong>PTO speed</strong> (540/1000) for the implement.\n- Engage PTO at <strong>low revs</strong> and build up; never at full throttle.\n- For the <strong>front + rear mower combination</strong>, both front and rear PTOs run — keep everyone well\n  clear of both ends (see <a href=\"#/read/hb-mowers\">D4</a>).</p>\n<p><strong>Transport / road</strong>\n- Brake pedals <strong>latched together</strong> for road.\n- Mind width/length through our gateways; beacon on; follow Module 03 road rules.</p>\n<p><strong>Slopes</strong>\n- Up/down not across, slow, no sharp turns, especially with a loader or full kit. Extra care on the\n  steeper ground (see field map).</p>\n<hr />\n<h2>Fuelling &amp; AdBlue</h2>\n<p>Engine off, Safe Stop, no smoking. Diesel in the main filler, <strong>AdBlue in the blue filler only</strong>.\nDon't let AdBlue run dry. See <a href=\"#/read/hb-fuel\">A1</a>.</p>\n<h2>Greasing &amp; operator maintenance</h2>\n<p>Safe Stop, key in pocket. Grease <strong>front and rear linkage pins, top link, pickup hitch, loader pins\nif fitted, and the PTO shaft joints + sliding tube</strong> of the implement in use. Daily oil/coolant\n(cold)/hydraulic/tyre checks; guards on. See <a href=\"#/read/hb-grease\">A2</a>.</p>\n<h2>Shutdown / parking</h2>\n<p>Firm level ground, lower linkage/loader, neutral/park, <strong>handbrake on, engine off, key out</strong>.</p>\n<hr />\n<h2>Quick do / don't</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>✅ Do</th>\n<th>❌ Don't</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Learn the controls before moving</td>\n<td>Guess which lever does what under load</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Seatbelt + handbrake every stop</td>\n<td>Rely on transmission to hold on a slope</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Front + rear mower: keep clear of both ends</td>\n<td>Stand near a running front mower</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>AdBlue in blue filler only</td>\n<td>Mix AdBlue and diesel</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Gentle PTO engagement</td>\n<td>Engage PTO at full throttle</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Sign-off:</strong> Trainee is competent to operate the John Deere 6150R under the supervision level set in Module 13.</p>\n<p>Trainee: <strong><em>_</em></strong><strong><em>_</em></strong><strong><em>_  Supervisor: </em></strong><strong><em>_</em></strong><strong><em>_</em></strong>_  Date: <strong><em>_</em></strong>___</p>"}, {"id": "hb-541", "group": "Machinery handbook", "short": "JCB 541-70 Loadall", "title": "C1 — JCB 541-70 Loadall (Telehandler)", "html": "<h1>C1 — JCB 541-70 Loadall (Telehandler)</h1>\n<p><em>Read <a href=\"#/read/telehandlers\">Module 04 — Telehandlers</a> first — the law, training and\nstability rules there are essential. This page adds the JCB 541-70 specifics.</em></p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Reminder:</strong> a 16-year-old may use this <strong>only on private farmland</strong>, only if <strong>trained\n(accredited Lantra/NPTC), competent, mature AND adequately supervised</strong> — and <strong>not on a public\nroad</strong>. This is our biggest, longest-reach machine; treat it with the most respect.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<hr />\n<h2>At a glance</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th></th>\n<th></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Type</td>\n<td>Agricultural telehandler (Loadall)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Max lift capacity</strong></td>\n<td><strong>~4,100 kg (4.1 t)</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Max lift height</strong></td>\n<td><strong>~7.0 m</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Capacity falls to</td>\n<td><strong>~1.5 t at full forward reach</strong>, ~2.25 t at full height</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Machine weight</td>\n<td>~8.2 t</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Fuel</td>\n<td>Diesel (+ AdBlue on newer models)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>The key safety fact:</strong> the load it can lift <strong>drops sharply as the boom goes up and out</strong>. 4.1 t is\nonly with the boom low and retracted. Always work to the <strong>load chart in the cab</strong>.</p>\n<hr />\n<h2>Pre-start daily checks</h2>\n<p><a href=\"#/read/hb-grease\">A2 daily checks</a>, plus:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>☐ Tyres (big, high-pressure) and wheel nuts.</li>\n<li>☐ <strong>Boom, hoses, rams</strong> — no leaks, no damage, no cracks.</li>\n<li>☐ <strong>Quick-hitch / headstock</strong>: attachment (bale spike or grab) <strong>locked and pinned</strong>.</li>\n<li>☐ <strong>Load chart</strong> present and readable; <strong>boom/load stability indicator</strong> working.</li>\n<li>☐ Brakes, steering, <strong>reversing alarm</strong>, beacon, horn, lights, mirrors/camera.</li>\n<li>☐ <strong>Seatbelt</strong> — wear it.</li>\n<li>☐ Forks/attachment correct for the job (see <a href=\"#/read/hb-grabs\">E1</a>).</li>\n</ul>\n<hr />\n<h2>Safe operation</h2>\n<p><strong>Start-up</strong>\n- Three points of contact in; <strong>seatbelt on</strong>; familiarise yourself with the joystick functions.</p>\n<p><strong>Picking up &amp; travelling</strong>\n- Approach the load square; engage the attachment fully.\n- <strong>Lift, then tilt back slightly</strong> to cradle the load.\n- <strong>Carry LOW</strong> — boom down and retracted — whenever travelling. A raised load while moving is the\n  classic way to tip a telehandler.\n- Travel and <strong>turn slowly</strong>; the machine is long and the back end swings wide.</p>\n<p><strong>Lifting &amp; placing</strong>\n- Position on <strong>firm, level ground</strong>, square to the stack.\n- Lift smoothly; <strong>watch the load chart and stability indicator</strong> — stop before any warning.\n- <strong>Never extend beyond the chart</strong> for the height/reach you're at.\n- Keep <strong>everyone out of the working area</strong> — nobody under or near a raised load.\n- Place on firm ground, lower, release, <strong>reverse out straight</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Overhead power lines (a telehandler killer)</strong>\n- The 7 m boom can reach lines a tractor never would, and <strong>electricity can arc across a gap</strong>.\n- <strong>Know where every line on the farm runs</strong> (mark them on the <a href=\"#/read/map\">field map</a>).\n- Plan stacking/tipping <strong>well clear</strong> of lines. If you contact one: <strong>stay in the cab</strong>, reverse/\n  lower clear if possible, keep everyone back, call for help (Module 08).</p>\n<p><strong>Slopes</strong>\n- Keep the <strong>load on the uphill side</strong>, travel up/down, load low. Avoid lifting on a slope.</p>\n<p><strong>Never</strong>\n- Never lift a person on the bare forks — only in a <strong>purpose-made, tested cage</strong> rated for this\n  machine.\n- Never use the machine beyond the load chart \"to save a second.\"</p>\n<hr />\n<h2>Fuelling</h2>\n<p>Engine off, Safe Stop, no smoking. Diesel (and AdBlue if fitted). See <a href=\"#/read/hb-fuel\">A1</a>.</p>\n<h2>Greasing &amp; operator maintenance</h2>\n<p>Safe Stop, <strong>boom down on the ground</strong>, key in pocket. Grease the <strong>boom pivot pins, ram pin ends,\nthe headstock/quick-hitch, and axle/steering pivots</strong> — there are many pins; follow the JCB grease\nschedule and bank. Check hydraulic oil, leaks, tyres, and that the load chart is legible.\nSee <a href=\"#/read/hb-grease\">A2</a>.</p>\n<h2>Shutdown / parking</h2>\n<p>Firm level ground, <strong>attachment flat on the ground, boom fully retracted and lowered</strong>, handbrake\non, engine off, key out. Never park with the boom up or a load raised.</p>\n<hr />\n<h2>Quick do / don't</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>✅ Do</th>\n<th>❌ Don't</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Work to the load chart</td>\n<td>Guess the weight / over-reach</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Travel with the load LOW, boom in</td>\n<td>Drive with a raised load</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Turn and travel slowly</td>\n<td>Swing the long back end into things/people</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Plan work clear of power lines</td>\n<td>Raise the boom near overhead lines</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Lift people only in a tested cage</td>\n<td>Lift anyone on bare forks</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Park boom down, attachment on the ground</td>\n<td>Leave the boom raised</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Sign-off:</strong> Trainee is competent to operate the JCB 541-70 under the supervision level set in Module 13. <em>(Accredited certificate also required — record in Module 14.)</em></p>\n<p>Trainee: <strong><em>_</em></strong><strong><em>_</em></strong><strong><em>_  Supervisor: </em></strong><strong><em>_</em></strong><strong><em>_</em></strong>_  Date: <strong><em>_</em></strong>___</p>"}, {"id": "hb-516", "group": "Machinery handbook", "short": "JCB 516-40", "title": "C2 — JCB 516-40 (Compact Telehandler)", "html": "<h1>C2 — JCB 516-40 (Compact Telehandler)</h1>\n<p><em>Read <a href=\"#/read/telehandlers\">Module 04 — Telehandlers</a> and the <a href=\"#/read/hb-541\">JCB 541-70 page</a>\nfirst — the same telehandler rules apply. This page covers what's different about the compact 516-40.</em></p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Same legal position as the 541-70: <strong>private land only</strong>, trained/competent/supervised, <strong>not on\na public road</strong> for a 16-year-old.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<hr />\n<h2>At a glance</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th></th>\n<th></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Type</td>\n<td><strong>Compact</strong> telehandler</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Max lift capacity</strong></td>\n<td><strong>~1,600 kg (1.6 t)</strong> — through the full 4 m height</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Max lift height</strong></td>\n<td><strong>~4.0 m</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Capacity at reach</td>\n<td><strong>~1,000 kg at ~2 m forward reach</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Machine weight</td>\n<td>~3.4 t</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Why \"compact\" matters for safety:</strong> it's smaller, lighter and lifts <strong>less than half</strong> what the\n541-70 does, and only ~4 m high. It's handy in tight yards and lower buildings — but because it's\nlight, it can feel <strong>tippy</strong> with a load near its limit. <strong>Don't treat it like the big machine</strong> —\nits limits come sooner.</p>\n<hr />\n<h2>Pre-start daily checks</h2>\n<p>Same as the <a href=\"#/read/hb-541\">541-70</a>: tyres, boom/hoses/rams, <strong>attachment locked</strong>,\n<strong>load chart</strong>, brakes/steering/alarm/beacon/horn/lights, <strong>seatbelt</strong>, correct attachment.</p>\n<hr />\n<h2>Safe operation — the differences</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Lower limits:</strong> max ~1.6 t and ~4 m. Round/square bales of haylage are <strong>heavy and wet</strong> — check\n  the bale weight against the chart; the 516-40 will reach its limit faster than the 541-70.</li>\n<li><strong>Lighter machine = easier to tip:</strong> carry the load <strong>low</strong>, travel/turn <strong>slowly</strong>, and be extra\n  careful with the load raised or on a slope.</li>\n<li><strong>Tight spaces:</strong> great for low/old buildings, but mind the <strong>boom hitting beams/doorways</strong> and\n  people in confined yard areas — use the alarm/horn and go slow.</li>\n<li>Everything else — load chart, exclusion zone, overhead lines, no lifting people on bare forks,\n  reversing awareness — is <strong>exactly as the 541-70 page</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<hr />\n<h2>Fuelling</h2>\n<p>Engine off, Safe Stop, no smoking. Diesel (+ AdBlue if fitted). See <a href=\"#/read/hb-fuel\">A1</a>.</p>\n<h2>Greasing &amp; operator maintenance</h2>\n<p>Safe Stop, <strong>boom down</strong>, key in pocket. Grease <strong>boom pivot, ram pins, headstock/quick-hitch, axle\npivots</strong>. Check hydraulic level, leaks, tyres, load chart legible. See <a href=\"#/read/hb-grease\">A2</a>.</p>\n<h2>Shutdown / parking</h2>\n<p>Firm level ground, attachment flat on the ground, boom retracted and down, handbrake on, engine off,\nkey out.</p>\n<hr />\n<h2>Quick do / don't</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>✅ Do</th>\n<th>❌ Don't</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Respect the lower 1.6 t / 4 m limits</td>\n<td>Assume it lifts like the 541-70</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Check heavy haylage bales against the chart</td>\n<td>Pick up a bale heavier than the chart allows</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Carry low, turn slowly (it's light &amp; tippy)</td>\n<td>Rush with a raised load</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Mind the boom in low buildings/doorways</td>\n<td>Clout beams or people in tight yards</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Sign-off:</strong> Trainee is competent to operate the JCB 516-40 under the supervision level set in Module 13. <em>(Accredited certificate also required — record in Module 14.)</em></p>\n<p>Trainee: <strong><em>_</em></strong><strong><em>_</em></strong><strong><em>_  Supervisor: </em></strong><strong><em>_</em></strong><strong><em>_</em></strong>_  Date: <strong><em>_</em></strong>___</p>"}, {"id": "hb-tedder", "group": "Machinery handbook", "short": "Kuhn GF 8501 tedder", "title": "D1 — Kuhn GF 8501 (Rotary Tedder)", "html": "<h1>D1 — Kuhn GF 8501 (Rotary Tedder)</h1>\n<p><em>Read <a href=\"#/read/pto\">Module 05 — PTO &amp; field machinery</a> first. The tedder spreads and\nturns cut grass to dry it. It's a wide machine with fast-spinning tine rotors.</em></p>\n<hr />\n<h2>At a glance</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th></th>\n<th></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Type</td>\n<td>Mounted rotary tedder, <strong>~8.5 m working width</strong>, multiple rotors</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Driven by</td>\n<td>Tractor <strong>PTO</strong> (typically 540 rpm)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Main hazards</td>\n<td>Fast spinning tines flinging debris; <strong>folding wings</strong> (crush points); transport width; tines that <strong>coast</strong> after the PTO stops</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<hr />\n<h2>Pre-start daily checks</h2>\n<p><a href=\"#/read/hb-grease\">A2 daily checks</a> on the tractor, plus on the tedder:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>☐ <strong>Tines</strong> — present, not bent/broken/missing (flying tines are dangerous). Replace as needed.</li>\n<li>☐ <strong>PTO shaft</strong> — full guard + anti-rotation chains, both ends locked on.</li>\n<li>☐ <strong>Folding rams/locks</strong> — sound; hoses not chafed.</li>\n<li>☐ Rotor gearboxes/arms — no obvious damage; greased (see below).</li>\n<li>☐ Transport latches/locks for the road.</li>\n</ul>\n<hr />\n<h2>Safe operation</h2>\n<p><strong>Hitching</strong>\n- Hitch on firm, level ground, engine off when connecting the <strong>PTO shaft</strong>; check guard and chains.\n- Connect hydraulics for folding; know which lever folds/unfolds.</p>\n<p><strong>Unfolding / folding</strong>\n- <strong>Keep everyone clear of the folding path</strong> — the wings are heavy crush hazards.\n- Fold/unfold exactly as the manual shows; engage <strong>transport locks</strong> before the road.</p>\n<p><strong>In work</strong>\n- Engage PTO at <strong>low revs</strong>, build up gently.\n- The tines <strong>throw grass, stones and debris</strong> — keep bystanders well clear; wear <strong>eye protection</strong>\n  when working near it.\n- Watch your <strong>8.5 m width</strong> — through gateways, near hedges, fences and the sand fencing (see field\n  map), and around field obstacles.\n- On slopes and rough ground, go at a sensible speed.</p>\n<p><strong>Blockages / tangled grass</strong>\n- <strong>Safe Stop</strong> (PTO disengaged, engine off, key out) and <strong>wait for the rotors to stop</strong> — they\n  coast for several seconds. Then clear, using a tool, watching for spring-back. Never clear it\n  running (Module 05).</p>\n<p><strong>Transport</strong>\n- Folded, locked, within legal width/height; lights/markers visible; beacon on.</p>\n<hr />\n<h2>Greasing &amp; operator maintenance</h2>\n<p>Safe Stop, key in pocket. Grease the <strong>rotor arm pivots, wheel/axle pivots, and the folding ram\npins</strong>, plus the <strong>PTO shaft joints and sliding tube</strong> — to Kuhn's schedule. Check tines and guards\nbefore every use. See <a href=\"#/read/hb-grease\">A2</a>.</p>\n<h2>Shutdown / parking</h2>\n<p>Lower to the ground (or park folded as designed) on firm level ground; Safe Stop.</p>\n<hr />\n<h2>Quick do / don't</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>✅ Do</th>\n<th>❌ Don't</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Eye protection; keep bystanders clear</td>\n<td>Stand near spinning/throwing tines</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Keep clear of folding wings</td>\n<td>Be under a folding wing</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Wait for rotors to stop before clearing</td>\n<td>Unblock with the PTO turning</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Mind the 8.5 m width &amp; transport locks</td>\n<td>Travel unfolded/unlatched on the road</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Replace bent/broken tines</td>\n<td>Run with missing/damaged tines</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Sign-off:</strong> Trainee is competent to operate the Kuhn GF 8501 tedder under the supervision level set in Module 13.</p>\n<p>Trainee: <strong><em>_</em></strong><strong><em>_</em></strong><strong><em>_  Supervisor: </em></strong><strong><em>_</em></strong><strong><em>_</em></strong>_  Date: <strong><em>_</em></strong>___</p>"}, {"id": "hb-rake", "group": "Machinery handbook", "short": "Kuhn GA twin rotor rake", "title": "D2 — Kuhn GA Twin Rotor Rake", "html": "<h1>D2 — Kuhn GA Twin Rotor Rake</h1>\n<p><em>Read <a href=\"#/read/pto\">Module 05 — PTO &amp; field machinery</a> first. The rake gathers the\ndried/wilted grass into rows (\"windrows\") ready for the baler. Two rotors with sprung tine arms\nsweep the grass together.</em></p>\n<hr />\n<h2>At a glance</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th></th>\n<th></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Type</td>\n<td>Trailed/mounted <strong>twin-rotor rake</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Driven by</td>\n<td>Tractor <strong>PTO</strong> + hydraulics for lift/fold</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Main hazards</td>\n<td><strong>Rotating tine arms</strong> at body height; <strong>folding rotors</strong> (crush + overhead reach); tines that coast on; transport width/height</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<hr />\n<h2>Pre-start daily checks</h2>\n<p><a href=\"#/read/hb-grease\">A2 daily checks</a>, plus:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>☐ <strong>Tine arms &amp; tines</strong> — secure, none bent/broken/missing; <strong>tine guards</strong> in place.</li>\n<li>☐ <strong>PTO shaft</strong> — full guard + chains, locked both ends.</li>\n<li>☐ <strong>Folding rams, hoses, locks</strong> — sound.</li>\n<li>☐ Rotor height/working width set correctly.</li>\n</ul>\n<hr />\n<h2>Safe operation</h2>\n<p><strong>Hitching &amp; folding</strong>\n- Connect PTO with the <strong>engine off</strong>; check guard/chains.\n- When <strong>folding the rotors up</strong>, they swing <strong>wide and high</strong> — keep clear of the folding path, and\n  <strong>watch for overhead power lines</strong> when rotors are raised for transport (see field map / Module 08).</p>\n<p><strong>In work</strong>\n- Engage PTO at <strong>low revs</strong>.\n- The <strong>tine arms sweep round at body height</strong> — never reach in or stand close while it turns.\n- Keep bystanders clear; thrown debris and a flicked tine can injure.\n- Mind ground speed on slopes and around obstacles.</p>\n<p><strong>Blockages / wrapped grass</strong>\n- <strong>Safe Stop</strong>, <strong>wait for the rotors and tine arms to stop</strong> (they coast), then clear with a tool.\n  Watch for sprung tine arms snapping back. Never clear it running.</p>\n<p><strong>Transport</strong>\n- Rotors folded and <strong>locked</strong>; within legal width/height; lights/markers; beacon on.</p>\n<hr />\n<h2>Greasing &amp; operator maintenance</h2>\n<p>Safe Stop, key in pocket. Grease the <strong>rotor bearings/cam track points, tine-arm pivots, wheel\npivots and folding ram pins</strong> to Kuhn's schedule, plus the <strong>PTO shaft</strong>. Check tines, guards and\nfolding locks. See <a href=\"#/read/hb-grease\">A2</a>.</p>\n<h2>Shutdown / parking</h2>\n<p>Lower rotors to the ground on firm level ground (or park folded as designed); Safe Stop.</p>\n<hr />\n<h2>Quick do / don't</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>✅ Do</th>\n<th>❌ Don't</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Stand well clear of the turning tine arms</td>\n<td>Reach toward a turning rotor</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Watch power lines when rotors are raised</td>\n<td>Fold rotors up under a power line</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Wait for everything to stop before clearing</td>\n<td>Pull wrapped grass out while it runs</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Transport folded &amp; locked</td>\n<td>Travel with rotors unlatched</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Sign-off:</strong> Trainee is competent to operate the Kuhn GA twin rotor rake under the supervision level set in Module 13.</p>\n<p>Trainee: <strong><em>_</em></strong><strong><em>_</em></strong><strong><em>_  Supervisor: </em></strong><strong><em>_</em></strong><strong><em>_</em></strong>_  Date: <strong><em>_</em></strong>___</p>"}, {"id": "hb-baler", "group": "Machinery handbook", "short": "New Holland BB940A baler", "title": "D3 — New Holland BB940A (Large Square Baler)", "html": "<h1>D3 — New Holland BB940A (Large Square Baler)</h1>\n<p><em>Read <a href=\"#/read/pto\">Module 05 — PTO &amp; field machinery</a> first. This is a </em><em>big square\nbaler</em><em> — a large, powerful machine with a pick-up, a plunger that rams at high force, knotters, and\na heavy bale chute. It demands the most care of any field machine here.</em></p>\n<hr />\n<h2>At a glance</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th></th>\n<th></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Type</td>\n<td>Large (big) square baler</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Bale size</td>\n<td>~<strong>80 × 90 cm</strong>, length ~1.2–2.5 m — bales are <strong>heavy</strong> (can be several hundred kg)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Power needed</td>\n<td>Big PTO — needs ~<strong>75 kW / 100 hp+</strong> (run with the Puma 240 CVX)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Knotting</td>\n<td>Twin/double knotters tie each bale</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Main hazards</td>\n<td><strong>Plunger</strong> (huge crushing force), <strong>pick-up</strong> entanglement, <strong>knotter</strong> area, <strong>flywheel</strong> momentum, <strong>heavy bale</strong> at the chute, <strong>fire</strong> (hot, dusty), <strong>needles/twine</strong> under tension</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<hr />\n<h2>Pre-start daily checks</h2>\n<p><a href=\"#/read/hb-grease\">A2 daily checks</a>, plus:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>☐ <strong>PTO shaft</strong> — full guard + chains, locked both ends.</li>\n<li>☐ <strong>All guards/covers</strong> on the plunger, knotter, drive and flywheel areas — <strong>none missing</strong>.</li>\n<li>☐ <strong>Pick-up</strong> tines/teeth condition; no build-up jamming it.</li>\n<li>☐ <strong>Twine</strong> correctly threaded (do this with the machine <strong>stopped</strong>) and enough on board.</li>\n<li>☐ <strong>Knotters</strong> clean; needles move freely (per manual).</li>\n<li>☐ Bale chute/ramp sound; <strong>fire extinguisher</strong> on the tractor/baler; blow off chaff build-up.</li>\n</ul>\n<hr />\n<h2>Safe operation</h2>\n<p><strong>Hitching</strong>\n- Hitch to the Puma on firm, level ground; connect the <strong>big PTO</strong> with the <strong>engine off</strong>; check\n  guard and chains; connect hydraulics/electrics for the monitor.</p>\n<p><strong>Starting baling</strong>\n- Clear the area of people.\n- Engage the PTO at <strong>low revs</strong> — the <strong>flywheel stores huge energy</strong> — then build up to working\n  revs. Let the plunger settle into rhythm.\n- Drive to keep a steady, even feed; don't overload the pick-up.</p>\n<p><strong>Critical danger zones — keep out while running:</strong>\n- <strong>Pick-up</strong> — never reach in; it grabs and pulls.\n- <strong>Plunger / bale chamber</strong> — the plunger rams with enough force to <strong>kill instantly</strong>. Never put a\n  hand or tool into the chamber while running, and <strong>never</strong> while the flywheel could still turn.\n- <strong>Knotter &amp; needles</strong> — under spring tension; <strong>never</strong> poke at a knotter or pull twine while it\n  runs.\n- <strong>Bale chute</strong> — the <strong>heavy finished bale</strong> moves out under power; <strong>stand clear of the rear</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Blockages — the most dangerous moment</strong>\n1. <strong>Safe Stop</strong> — PTO disengaged, engine off, <strong>key out and in your pocket</strong>.\n2. <strong>Wait for the flywheel and all parts to come to a complete stop</strong> — the flywheel coasts a long\n   time.\n3. Release any <strong>stored energy / tension</strong> carefully (per manual). Use a tool, not your hands.\n4. Replace guards, stand clear, restart at low revs.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Many baler deaths happen when someone clears a chamber or pick-up with the machine running, or\nbefore the flywheel stops. <strong>There is no blockage worth your arm or your life.</strong></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>Slopes</strong>\n- The baler is <strong>tall and heavy</strong> — go slow, avoid holes and drop-offs, no sharp turns across a slope\n  (it can tip). Take care on Sands Hill / The Hill and uneven ground.</p>\n<p><strong>Fire watch</strong>\n- Balers run <strong>hot and dusty</strong> — a real fire risk in dry hay. Blow off chaff from hot parts; keep the\n  <strong>extinguisher</strong> to hand; carry a phone with the <a href=\"#/read/emergency-card\">emergency card</a>;\n  watch for hot bearings / burning smell.</p>\n<hr />\n<h2>Greasing &amp; operator maintenance</h2>\n<p>Safe Stop, key in pocket, <strong>flywheel stopped</strong>. The BB940A has <strong>many grease points</strong> — pick-up,\nplunger slides, knotter mechanism, chamber rollers, drive — often grouped on <strong>grease banks</strong> and\nsome on <strong>auto-lube</strong>. Follow the New Holland schedule and the bank layout your supervisor shows you.\nKeep the knotter area clean. Replace all guards before running. See <a href=\"#/read/hb-grease\">A2</a>.</p>\n<h2>Shutdown / parking</h2>\n<p>On firm level ground; lower as designed; Safe Stop; let it cool; never leave it running unattended.</p>\n<hr />\n<h2>Quick do / don't</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>✅ Do</th>\n<th>❌ Don't</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Engage PTO at low revs (flywheel!)</td>\n<td>Slam the PTO in at full throttle</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Stand clear of the rear when a bale ejects</td>\n<td>Stand behind during bale discharge</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Safe Stop + wait for flywheel to STOP before clearing</td>\n<td>Reach into pick-up/chamber/knotter running</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Thread twine with the machine stopped</td>\n<td>Feed/pull twine while it runs</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Keep extinguisher to hand, blow off chaff</td>\n<td>Ignore a hot/burning smell</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Slow on slopes (tall &amp; heavy)</td>\n<td>Turn sharply across a slope</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Sign-off:</strong> Trainee is competent to operate the New Holland BB940A under the supervision level set in Module 13. <em>(Given the hazards, expect extended direct supervision on this machine.)</em></p>\n<p>Trainee: <strong><em>_</em></strong><strong><em>_</em></strong><strong><em>_  Supervisor: </em></strong><strong><em>_</em></strong><strong><em>_</em></strong>_  Date: <strong><em>_</em></strong>___</p>"}, {"id": "hb-mowers", "group": "Machinery handbook", "short": "Kuhn 3m mowers", "title": "D4 — Kuhn Front &amp; Rear 3 m Mowers (Disc Mower Combination)", "html": "<h1>D4 — Kuhn Front &amp; Rear 3 m Mowers (Disc Mower Combination)</h1>\n<p><em>Read <a href=\"#/read/pto\">Module 05 — PTO &amp; field machinery</a> first. We run a </em><em>front-mounted\n3 m mower</em><em> and a </em><em>rear-mounted 3 m mower</em><em> together (a \"combination\", ~6 m cut). Disc mowers have\nrazor-sharp discs/blades spinning extremely fast — the biggest \"thrown object\" risk on the farm.</em></p>\n<hr />\n<h2>At a glance</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th></th>\n<th></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Type</td>\n<td>Front + rear mounted <strong>disc mowers</strong>, ~3 m each (~6 m combined)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Driven by</td>\n<td><strong>Front PTO</strong> (front mower) <strong>and rear PTO</strong> (rear mower) — both running together</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Main hazards</td>\n<td><strong>Razor-sharp fast discs</strong>; <strong>thrown stones/objects/broken blades</strong> (can travel a long way); two cutting units front and rear; discs that <strong>coast</strong> after the PTO stops; folding for transport</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<hr />\n<h2>Pre-start daily checks</h2>\n<p><a href=\"#/read/hb-grease\">A2 daily checks</a>, plus on <strong>each</strong> mower:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>☐ <strong>Blades/knives</strong> — present, sharp, not cracked or worn thin; <strong>replace damaged blades as a set</strong>\n  with the correct bolts/pins (a flying blade is lethal).</li>\n<li>☐ <strong>Disc/skid condition</strong> and cutterbar — no damage.</li>\n<li>☐ <strong>Protective curtains/guards/covers</strong> around the cutterbar — <strong>all in place</strong> (these stop\n  thrown objects).</li>\n<li>☐ <strong>PTO shafts (front and rear)</strong> — full guards + chains, locked on.</li>\n<li>☐ <strong>Breakback</strong> device free (lets the mower swing back if it hits an obstacle).</li>\n<li>☐ Folding rams/locks and hoses sound.</li>\n</ul>\n<hr />\n<h2>Safe operation</h2>\n<p><strong>Before cutting — walk the field</strong>\n- Disc mowers throw whatever they hit. <strong>Check the field for stones, debris, wire, posts and hidden\n  obstacles</strong>, and for people/animals, before you start.</p>\n<p><strong>Hitching</strong>\n- Connect <strong>front and rear PTOs</strong> with the <strong>engine off</strong>; check both guards and chains.</p>\n<p><strong>In work</strong>\n- Engage PTO at <strong>low revs</strong>, build up gently. Both units come up to speed.\n- <strong>Keep everyone well clear — front AND rear, and especially out of the line of throw.</strong> Never let\n  anyone stand in front of, beside, or behind the mowers while running.\n- Lower the cutterbars only when clear; raise at headlands.\n- Mind the ~6 m total width and the front unit's reach in gateways and near hedges/fences.\n- On slopes, go steady (Module 03).</p>\n<p><strong>Hitting an obstacle</strong>\n- If a mower hits something, the <strong>breakback</strong> lets it swing back — <strong>stop, Safe Stop, and inspect</strong>\n  for damaged blades before continuing. A cracked blade can fly off.</p>\n<p><strong>Blockages / clearing grass</strong>\n- <strong>Safe Stop</strong> (both PTOs disengaged, engine off, key out) and <strong>wait for the discs to STOP</strong> — they\n  coast for several seconds and the blades are razor-sharp. Then clear with a tool and gloves.\n  Never near a running or coasting disc.</p>\n<p><strong>Transport</strong>\n- Fold and <strong>lock</strong> as designed; guards/curtains down; within legal width/height; lights/beacon.</p>\n<hr />\n<h2>Greasing &amp; operator maintenance</h2>\n<p>Safe Stop, key in pocket, <strong>discs stopped</strong>. Grease <strong>pivot points, folding ram pins and the\nfront + rear PTO shaft joints/sliding tubes</strong>; check the <strong>cutterbar oil level</strong> per Kuhn's manual.\n<strong>Always replace the protective curtains/guards before running.</strong> Inspect blades and fixings daily.\nSee <a href=\"#/read/hb-grease\">A2</a>.</p>\n<h2>Shutdown / parking</h2>\n<p>Lower cutterbars to the ground on firm level ground (or park folded as designed); Safe Stop.</p>\n<hr />\n<h2>Quick do / don't</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>✅ Do</th>\n<th>❌ Don't</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Walk the field for stones/debris first</td>\n<td>Mow over unknown debris/wire</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Keep everyone clear, front and rear, out of throw line</td>\n<td>Let anyone stand near running mowers</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Guards/curtains in place before running</td>\n<td>Run with a curtain/guard removed</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Replace damaged blades as a set</td>\n<td>Carry on with a cracked blade</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Wait for discs to fully stop before clearing</td>\n<td>Touch a coasting/running disc</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Fold &amp; lock for transport</td>\n<td>Travel unfolded/unlatched</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Sign-off:</strong> Trainee is competent to operate the Kuhn front &amp; rear 3 m mower combination under the supervision level set in Module 13.</p>\n<p>Trainee: <strong><em>_</em></strong><strong><em>_</em></strong><strong><em>_  Supervisor: </em></strong><strong><em>_</em></strong><strong><em>_</em></strong>_  Date: <strong><em>_</em></strong>___</p>"}, {"id": "hb-grabs", "group": "Machinery handbook", "short": "Bale grabs & squeeze", "title": "E1 — Bale Grabs &amp; Squeeze (Telehandler Attachments)", "html": "<h1>E1 — Bale Grabs &amp; Squeeze (Telehandler Attachments)</h1>\n<p><em>Read <a href=\"#/read/telehandlers\">Module 04 — Telehandlers</a> and the telehandler pages\n(<a href=\"#/read/hb-541\">C1</a>, <a href=\"#/read/hb-516\">C2</a>) first. These are the\nattachments that pick up and stack bales — using the </em><em>right</em><em> one, fitted correctly, is what keeps\nbales from rolling back onto the operator.</em></p>\n<hr />\n<h2>At a glance</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th></th>\n<th></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Bale grab / grapple</td>\n<td>Hydraulic arms that <strong>grip</strong> a bale (or several) from above/around</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Bale squeeze</td>\n<td>Plates that <strong>clamp</strong> bales from the sides</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Spike (for reference)</td>\n<td>A spear into a round bale (Module 06 — never use the plain bucket for bales)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Covered by</td>\n<td><strong>LOLER</strong> — lifting attachments must be inspected; <strong>PUWER</strong> — suitable &amp; maintained</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Main hazards</td>\n<td>A <strong>dropped/rolling bale</strong>; the <strong>hydraulic clamp/crush</strong> of the arms; an attachment <strong>not properly locked</strong> on the headstock falling off</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<hr />\n<h2>Before use</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>☐ Correct attachment for the bale type (round vs square; grab vs squeeze).</li>\n<li>☐ <strong>Fitted and locked</strong> onto the telehandler quick-hitch — <strong>pins/locks engaged</strong>, hydraulic\n  couplers connected and clean.</li>\n<li>☐ Arms/plates, pivots and rams sound; <strong>no cracks, no leaks</strong>.</li>\n<li>☐ <strong>LOLER inspection in date</strong> (your supervisor keeps the record) — a lifting attachment must be\n  examined periodically by law.</li>\n<li>☐ Hydraulic function tested (open/close) before lifting a real bale.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Always confirm the quick-hitch is locked.</strong> An attachment that drops off under load is a serious\nfalling-object hazard.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<hr />\n<h2>Safe use</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Approach the bale square; <strong>grip/clamp firmly</strong> before lifting — make sure it's secure and won't\n  slip or roll.</li>\n<li><strong>Carry low</strong>, boom retracted; travel and turn slowly (telehandler stability — see <a href=\"#/read/hb-541\">C1</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Keep everyone clear</strong> of the load and the machine's path. A bale that slips from a grab/squeeze\n  falls fast and <strong>rolls</strong> — it can crush.</li>\n<li>Watch the <strong>load weight</strong> vs the telehandler's chart — wet haylage bales are heavy, and the grab/\n  squeeze itself adds weight and changes the load centre.</li>\n<li>Place bales gently and squarely when stacking (Module 06 — bonded, stable, not too high, clear of\n  power lines and edges). Round bales <strong>roll</strong> — chock or stack so they can't run away, especially on\n  any slope.</li>\n<li>Release only when the bale is <strong>stable and supported</strong> on the stack/ground.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Crush hazard from the attachment itself</strong>\n- The arms/plates close with <strong>hydraulic force</strong> — keep <strong>hands and people out</strong> of the closing area.\n- Never reach between the arms/plates or between attachment and bale while the engine is running.</p>\n<hr />\n<h2>Maintenance</h2>\n<p>Safe Stop, attachment lowered to the ground, key in pocket. Grease the <strong>arm/plate pivots and ram\npin ends</strong>; check hydraulic hoses/couplers for leaks; check the <strong>quick-hitch locking</strong> mechanism\nworks. Report any damage and keep the <strong>LOLER inspection</strong> up to date.\nSee <a href=\"#/read/hb-grease\">A2</a>.</p>\n<hr />\n<h2>Quick do / don't</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>✅ Do</th>\n<th>❌ Don't</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Confirm attachment locked on the hitch</td>\n<td>Lift with an unlocked attachment</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Grip/clamp firmly, check secure before lifting</td>\n<td>Lift a loosely held bale</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Keep people clear of load &amp; rolling path</td>\n<td>Let anyone near a suspended bale</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Mind heavy haylage vs the load chart</td>\n<td>Over-reach / overload</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Keep hands out of the closing arms</td>\n<td>Reach between arms/plates while running</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Keep LOLER inspection in date</td>\n<td>Use an out-of-test lifting attachment</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Sign-off:</strong> Trainee can fit and use the bale grab/squeeze safely under the supervision level set in Module 13.</p>\n<p>Trainee: <strong><em>_</em></strong><strong><em>_</em></strong><strong><em>_  Supervisor: </em></strong><strong><em>_</em></strong><strong><em>_</em></strong>_  Date: <strong><em>_</em></strong>___</p>"}, {"id": "hb-trailers", "group": "Machinery handbook", "short": "Bale trailers", "title": "E2 — Bale Trailers", "html": "<h1>E2 — Bale Trailers</h1>\n<p><em>Read <a href=\"#/read/tractors\">Module 03 — Tractors (Trailers section)</a> first. Bale trailers carry the\nfinished bales from field to stack. The dangers are an </em><em>unstable load</em><em>, </em><em>bales falling</em><em>, </em><em>people\nriding or standing in the wrong place</em><em>, and </em><em>hitching/tipping</em><em> mistakes.</em></p>\n<hr />\n<h2>At a glance</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th></th>\n<th></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Type</td>\n<td>Flat / bale trailer, towed behind the tractor</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Main hazards</td>\n<td>Load <strong>toppling or sliding</strong>; <strong>bales falling</strong> when loading/unloading/in transit; <strong>runaway</strong> trailer; <strong>overturning</strong> (top-heavy load, slopes, speed); crush while <strong>hitching</strong>; tipping into <strong>overhead lines</strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<hr />\n<h2>Before use</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>☐ <strong>Hitch / drawbar eye</strong> sound; correct size for the tractor pickup hitch.</li>\n<li>☐ <strong>Brakes</strong> (if braked) and <strong>lights/indicators</strong> working — connect the lines.</li>\n<li>☐ <strong>Breakaway cable / safety chain</strong> present and connected.</li>\n<li>☐ Tyres and wheel nuts good; bed clear of loose debris.</li>\n<li>☐ Within the <strong>load rating</strong> of trailer and tractor — don't overload.</li>\n</ul>\n<hr />\n<h2>Hitching (a crush danger)</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Reverse up squarely; <strong>Safe Stop</strong> before getting between tractor and trailer.</li>\n<li><strong>Never stand between the tractor and trailer while the engine is running.</strong> Many people are\n  crushed at the hitch.</li>\n<li>Lock the hitch, fit the <strong>safety pin/clip</strong>, connect <strong>brakes, lights and breakaway cable</strong>, raise\n  the jack/stand.</li>\n</ul>\n<hr />\n<h2>Loading &amp; securing</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Load <strong>evenly and low</strong> where possible; build the load <strong>stable and bonded</strong> so it can't shift or\n  topple — like stacking (Module 06).</li>\n<li>Don't build the load <strong>too high</strong> for the trailer's width — top-heavy loads overturn.</li>\n<li><strong>Secure the load</strong> as required (straps/net) so nothing falls in transit.</li>\n<li><strong>Round bales roll</strong> — load so they can't roll off; chock if needed.</li>\n<li>Keep <strong>clear of the trailer</strong> while the telehandler loads it — never stand on or beside the bed in\n  the loading zone (falling bales).</li>\n</ul>\n<hr />\n<h2>Towing</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>No passengers</strong> in or on the trailer or load — <strong>ever</strong>.</li>\n<li>Drive to suit the load: <strong>slow on bends, slopes and rough ground</strong>; a loaded bale trailer is heavy\n  and can push or overturn the tractor.</li>\n<li>Allow extra <strong>braking distance</strong>; check brakes early on a hill.</li>\n<li>Mind <strong>width, length and height</strong> through our gateways and lanes (see field map), and watch for\n  <strong>overhead lines</strong> with a high load.</li>\n<li>Take special care on the steeper ground (Sands Hill, The Hill).</li>\n</ul>\n<hr />\n<h2>Unloading / tipping</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Unload on <strong>firm, level ground</strong>.</li>\n<li>If the trailer tips: tip only on level ground, <strong>look up for overhead power lines first</strong>, keep\n  people clear, and <strong>never go under a raised body</strong> — prop it if you must work near it.</li>\n<li>Watch for the load <strong>shifting or rolling</strong> as straps come off — stand clear.</li>\n</ul>\n<hr />\n<h2>Parking</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Park on firm, level ground; chock the wheels; lower the jack/stand; if unhitched, ensure it can't\n  roll. Safe Stop the tractor.</li>\n</ul>\n<hr />\n<h2>Greasing &amp; maintenance</h2>\n<p>Grease the <strong>hitch eye, brake linkages, tipping ram pins and axle points</strong>; check brakes, lights and\ntyres. Report worn hitches, dud lights or poor brakes — these are safety-critical. See <a href=\"#/read/hb-grease\">A2</a>.</p>\n<hr />\n<h2>Quick do / don't</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>✅ Do</th>\n<th>❌ Don't</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Connect brakes, lights, breakaway cable</td>\n<td>Tow without lights/brakes/safety chain</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Safe Stop before going to the hitch</td>\n<td>Stand between tractor &amp; trailer running</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Load even, low, stable, secured</td>\n<td>Build a tall top-heavy load</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Keep clear while it's being loaded</td>\n<td>Stand on the bed during loading</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Look up for lines before tipping</td>\n<td>Tip under/near overhead power lines</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Slow on slopes &amp; bends</td>\n<td>Carry passengers on the trailer/load</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Sign-off:</strong> Trainee can hitch, load, tow and unload a bale trailer safely under the supervision level set in Module 13.</p>\n<p>Trainee: <strong><em>_</em></strong><strong><em>_</em></strong><strong><em>_  Supervisor: </em></strong><strong><em>_</em></strong><strong><em>_</em></strong>_  Date: <strong><em>_</em></strong>___</p>"}, {"id": "map", "group": "Reference", "short": "Farm map & field names", "title": "Module 16 — Farm Map &amp; Field Names", "html": "<h1>Module 16 — Farm Map &amp; Field Names</h1>\n<p><em>Knowing where you are, what the field is called, and where the hazards are is part of working\nsafely. This map is also your </em><em>induction markup exercise</em><em> (Module 01) — print it and mark the\nhazards on it with your supervisor.</em></p>\n<hr />\n<h2>Truleigh Manor Farm — field map</h2>\n<p><img alt=\"Truleigh Manor Farm field map\" src=\"assets/farm-map.png\" /></p>\n<p><em>Numbers 1–33 are the fields (see the key below). The bold line is the farm boundary; dashed lines\nare paths/footpaths. A full-resolution vector version for printing is at\n<a href=\"assets/farm-map.svg\"><code>Farm-Map/truleigh-manor-farm-map.svg</code></a>. The original source\nmap is <a href=\"#/read/map\"><code>Farm-Map/Truleigh_Manor_Farm_Field_Maps.kml</code></a>\n(open in Google Earth).</em></p>\n<hr />\n<h2>Field names key</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>#</th>\n<th>Field</th>\n<th>#</th>\n<th>Field</th>\n<th>#</th>\n<th>Field</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>Hither Cowfield</td>\n<td>12</td>\n<td>North East Sands</td>\n<td>23</td>\n<td>West Lane</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>East Cowfield</td>\n<td>13</td>\n<td>Sands Hill</td>\n<td>24</td>\n<td>The Hill</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>West Cowfield</td>\n<td>14</td>\n<td>The Sands</td>\n<td>25</td>\n<td>East Lane</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>The Fletchetts</td>\n<td>15</td>\n<td>Wood Field</td>\n<td>26</td>\n<td>Browns Meadow</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>South Flacketts</td>\n<td>16</td>\n<td>Airstrip</td>\n<td>27</td>\n<td>OX Pasture</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>6</td>\n<td>North Flacketts</td>\n<td>17</td>\n<td>Nine Acre</td>\n<td>28</td>\n<td>The Crofts</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>7</td>\n<td>West Sands</td>\n<td>18</td>\n<td>East Wood</td>\n<td>29</td>\n<td>Airstrip Meadow</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>8</td>\n<td>Banky Sands</td>\n<td>19</td>\n<td>Wingfield</td>\n<td>30</td>\n<td>The Brooks</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>9</td>\n<td>West Meadow</td>\n<td>20</td>\n<td>Gratlands</td>\n<td>31</td>\n<td>North Furze Wood</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>10</td>\n<td>The Lakes</td>\n<td>21</td>\n<td>Gratlands Paddocks</td>\n<td>32</td>\n<td>South Furze Wood</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>11</td>\n<td>East Meadow</td>\n<td>22</td>\n<td>Garden Paddock</td>\n<td>33</td>\n<td>Williams</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Use field names/numbers on the radio/phone so everyone knows exactly where you are — vital if you\never need to direct help to you (Module 09).</p>\n</blockquote>\n<hr />\n<h2>Markup exercise — do this at induction (with your supervisor)</h2>\n<p>Print the map (or the SVG) and <strong>mark on it</strong>, walking the farm where you can:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>☐ <strong>Overhead power lines</strong> and their routes — and any low ones. <em>(Critical for telehandler,\n  rake-folding and tipping work — Modules 04, 08.)</em></li>\n<li>☐ <strong>Steep slopes / banky ground</strong> where tractors and loaded trailers could overturn\n  (e.g. around Sands Hill, The Hill, Banky Sands).</li>\n<li>☐ <strong>Water</strong> — The Lakes, The Brooks, ponds, ditches and any sand/coastal water features.</li>\n<li>☐ <strong>Soft / boggy ground</strong> that could bog or tip a machine.</li>\n<li>☐ <strong>Fragile-roof buildings</strong> and structures <strong>never to climb on</strong> (Module 08).</li>\n<li>☐ <strong>No-go areas</strong> — slurry stores, tanks, pits, grain stores / confined spaces (Module 08).</li>\n<li>☐ <strong>Main gateways and pinch points</strong> (narrow gates — mind machine width; note the 10 ft &amp; 12 ft\n  gates) and the <strong>public footpath</strong> (members of the public about — Module 08).</li>\n<li>☐ The <strong>hay/haylage route</strong> from field → yard → stack, and where bales are stacked.</li>\n<li>☐ The <strong>fuel/chemical store</strong>.</li>\n<li>☐ The <strong>assembly point</strong>, <strong>first aid kit</strong> and <strong>fire extinguisher</strong> locations.</li>\n<li>☐ The <strong>two emergency locations</strong> for 999 (see card below): <strong>farm entrance</strong> and <strong>Truleigh\n  Sands</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Keep the marked-up copy with this pack and review it if anything changes.</p>\n<hr />\n<h2>Emergency locations (for 999 / what3words)</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Place</th>\n<th>what3words</th>\n<th>Link</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Farm entrance</strong></td>\n<td><code>///chicken.airless.hazy</code></td>\n<td>https://w3w.co/chicken.airless.hazy</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Truleigh Sands</strong></td>\n<td><code>///playback.poker.touched</code></td>\n<td>https://w3w.co/playback.poker.touched</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Full emergency details are on the <a href=\"#/read/emergency-card\">Emergency Information Card</a> — print\nit and keep it in the cab.</p>\n<hr />\n<h2>Regenerating the map</h2>\n<p>If field boundaries change, update the KML and re-run:</p>\n<pre><code>python3 tools/render_farm_map.py\n</code></pre>\n<p>This rewrites <code>Farm-Map/truleigh-manor-farm-map.svg</code> and <code>.png</code>.</p>"}, {"id": "risk", "group": "Reference", "short": "Young-person risk assessment", "title": "Module 12 — Young Person Risk Assessment (Template)", "html": "<h1>Module 12 — Young Person Risk Assessment (Template)</h1>\n<p><em>Complete this </em><em>before</em><em> the young person starts work. It is a legal requirement under the Management of Health and Safety at Work Regulations 1999 (reg. 19) and must take account of the young person's inexperience, immaturity and lack of awareness of risk. Review it whenever tasks, equipment or the worker's experience change.</em></p>\n<hr />\n<h2>Details</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th></th>\n<th></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Business / farm</td>\n<td>Truleigh Manor Farm</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Assessment carried out by</td>\n<td><strong><em>_</em></strong><strong><em>_</em></strong><strong><em>_</em></strong>____</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Date</td>\n<td><strong><em>_</em></strong><strong><em>_</em></strong><strong><em>_</em></strong>____</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Young person's name</td>\n<td><strong><em>_</em></strong><strong><em>_</em></strong><strong><em>_</em></strong>____</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Tasks they will do</td>\n<td>Haymaking &amp; haylage: tractor driving, tedding, raking, baling, wrapping, carting, telehandler/bale handling, general yard work</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Supervisor(s)</td>\n<td><strong><em>_</em></strong><strong><em>_</em></strong><strong><em>_</em></strong>____</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<hr />\n<h2>How to use the grid</h2>\n<p>For each hazard: rate the <strong>risk before controls</strong> (Low / Med / High), list the <strong>controls</strong> you will actually put in place, then rate the <strong>residual risk</strong>. Add anything specific to your farm. The \"young person factors\" column is the legal extra you must consider — their inexperience and how you'll manage it.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>#</th>\n<th>Hazard</th>\n<th>Who's at risk &amp; how</th>\n<th>Risk (before)</th>\n<th>Controls (what you will do)</th>\n<th>Young-person factors</th>\n<th>Residual risk</th>\n<th>Action / by whom</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td><strong>Tractor — overturn/run-over</strong></td>\n<td>Crush, run-over, rollover</td>\n<td>High</td>\n<td>Trained &amp; signed off (Mod 03); seatbelt; Safe Stop; up/down slopes; no high hitching; no passengers; speed limits</td>\n<td>Inexperience on slopes/yard → direct supervision early; build up gradually</td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td><strong>Telehandler — tip/crush/falling load/lines</strong></td>\n<td>Operator &amp; bystanders</td>\n<td>High</td>\n<td>Accredited (Lantra/NPTC) training; load low; load chart; right attachment; lines mapped; direct supervision; LOLER-inspected attachments</td>\n<td>Immaturity/over-confidence with reach &amp; load → strict supervision; defer lone use</td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td><strong>PTO &amp; machinery entanglement</strong></td>\n<td>Operator</td>\n<td>High</td>\n<td>Full PTO guard + chains; no loose clothing; blockages cleared only after Safe Stop &amp; all motion stopped (Mod 05); guards never defeated</td>\n<td>Lack of awareness of coasting parts → drill the stop procedure; supervise</td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td><strong>Mower/tedder — thrown objects &amp; blades</strong></td>\n<td>Operator &amp; bystanders</td>\n<td>High</td>\n<td>Bystanders kept clear; eye protection; blades checked; wait for stop</td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5</td>\n<td><strong>Baler/wrapper — ejection, crush, rotating arm</strong></td>\n<td>Operator &amp; bystanders</td>\n<td>High</td>\n<td>Stand clear of rear/arm radius; no hand feeding; slow on slopes</td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>6</td>\n<td><strong>Bale handling &amp; stacking — falling/rolling bales, stack collapse</strong></td>\n<td>Operator &amp; those nearby</td>\n<td>High</td>\n<td>Correct attachment; carry low; exclusion zone; bonded stable stacks; no climbing stacks; round bales chocked</td>\n<td>Tendency to take shortcuts → supervise stacking; agree max stack height</td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>7</td>\n<td><strong>Manual handling</strong></td>\n<td>Back/musculoskeletal injury</td>\n<td>Med</td>\n<td>Use machines for heavy loads; good technique (Mod 07); split loads; get help</td>\n<td>Still developing physically → limit heavy lifting</td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>8</td>\n<td><strong>Noise</strong></td>\n<td>Permanent hearing loss</td>\n<td>Med</td>\n<td>Ear protection provided &amp; worn; doors shut</td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>9</td>\n<td><strong>Dust / mould (Farmer's Lung)</strong></td>\n<td>Lung disease</td>\n<td>Med</td>\n<td>FFP3 masks; work upwind; don't disturb mouldy hay unmasked</td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>10</td>\n<td><strong>Vibration (HAVS / whole-body)</strong></td>\n<td>Nerve, circulation, back damage</td>\n<td>Med</td>\n<td>Seat set for weight; limit tool time; report symptoms</td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>11</td>\n<td><strong>Heat / sun / dehydration</strong></td>\n<td>Heat illness, sunburn, skin cancer</td>\n<td>Med</td>\n<td>Water; breaks; sunscreen/hat; recognise heat exhaustion</td>\n<td>Won't always self-manage → supervisor prompts breaks/fluids</td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>12</td>\n<td><strong>Working at height / fragile roofs</strong></td>\n<td>Fatal falls</td>\n<td>High</td>\n<td>Avoid height; no climbing stacks/roofs; proper access only</td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>13</td>\n<td><strong>Overhead/underground electricity</strong></td>\n<td>Electrocution</td>\n<td>High</td>\n<td>Lines mapped; high-reach work kept clear; emergency procedure known</td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>14</td>\n<td><strong>Fire (machinery, fuel, hay heating)</strong></td>\n<td>Burns, property</td>\n<td>Med</td>\n<td>Extinguishers on machines; clear chaff; monitor stacks; no smoking</td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>15</td>\n<td><strong>Slurry gas / confined spaces</strong></td>\n<td>Asphyxiation, poisoning</td>\n<td>High</td>\n<td><strong>Prohibited</strong> for this worker; keep clear during agitation</td>\n<td>Must be an absolute no-go area</td>\n<td>Low</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>16</td>\n<td><strong>Livestock</strong></td>\n<td>Crush/trample</td>\n<td>Med/High</td>\n<td>Keep clear unless directed; escape routes; never alone if inexperienced</td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>17</td>\n<td><strong>Chemicals/fuel</strong></td>\n<td>Poisoning, burns</td>\n<td>Med</td>\n<td>No pesticide handling without certificate; PPE per label; wash before eating</td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>18</td>\n<td><strong>Fatigue / long hours / time pressure</strong></td>\n<td>Errors, all of the above</td>\n<td>High</td>\n<td>Reasonable hours; breaks; \"no rushing in the dark\"; can always say stop</td>\n<td>Young workers feel pressure to please → make stopping explicitly OK</td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>19</td>\n<td><strong>Lone working</strong></td>\n<td>Delayed help if hurt</td>\n<td>High</td>\n<td>Restrict lone working on high-risk tasks; check-in routine; phone &amp; location card</td>\n<td>Not for an inexperienced 16-yr-old on machinery initially</td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>20</td>\n<td><strong>Public roads</strong></td>\n<td>Collision</td>\n<td>High</td>\n<td>Road use only within licence/competence; width rules; hi-vis; many tasks kept off-road</td>\n<td>Defer road work until competent</td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><em>(Add farm-specific hazards: ponds, specific slopes, particular gateways, weak bridges, electric fences, etc.)</em></p>\n<p>| 21 | | | | | | | |\n| 22 | | | | | | | |</p>\n<hr />\n<h2>Working hours for under-18s (note)</h2>\n<p>Young workers (school-leaving age to 18) generally have limits on working hours and rest breaks under the Working Time Regulations (typically not more than 8 hours/day or 40/week, with daily and weekly rest), with limited exceptions in agriculture. <strong>Check current rules and agree reasonable hours</strong> — especially important during pressured haymaking. Fatigue is itself a major hazard (row 18).</p>\n<hr />\n<h2>Sign-off</h2>\n<p>I confirm this risk assessment has been completed, the controls are in place, and the young person has been informed of the risks and the controls.</p>\n<p>Assessor: <strong><em>_</em></strong><strong><em>_</em></strong><strong><em>_  Signature: </em></strong><strong><em>_</em></strong><strong><em>_</em></strong>_  Date: <strong><em>_</em></strong>___</p>\n<p>Young person — I have had the risks and controls explained to me and I understand them.</p>\n<p>Name: <strong><em>_</em></strong><strong><em>_</em></strong><strong><em>_  Signature: </em></strong><strong><em>_</em></strong><strong><em>_</em></strong>_  Date: <strong><em>_</em></strong>___</p>"}, {"id": "emergency-card", "group": "Reference", "short": "Emergency information card", "title": "⚠️ EMERGENCY INFORMATION CARD — Truleigh Manor Farm", "html": "<h1>⚠️ EMERGENCY INFORMATION CARD — Truleigh Manor Farm</h1>\n<p><em>Print this, laminate it, and keep a copy in every tractor/telehandler cab and in the trainee's\npocket. In an emergency, calm and clear information saves minutes — and minutes save lives.</em></p>\n<hr />\n<h2>IN AN EMERGENCY</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Make it safe</strong> — Safe Stop machines; don't become a second casualty (electricity, slurry gas,\n   machinery).</li>\n<li><strong>Check the casualty</strong> — responsive? breathing?</li>\n<li><strong>CALL 999</strong> (or <strong>112</strong>) — ask for <strong>AMBULANCE</strong> (and Fire/Police if needed).</li>\n<li><strong>Give first aid</strong> within your training.</li>\n<li><strong>Send someone to the gate to guide the ambulance in.</strong></li>\n</ol>\n<hr />\n<h2>SAY THIS TO 999</h2>\n<blockquote>\n<p>\"Ambulance to <strong>Truleigh Manor Farm, Edburton Road, Henfield, BN5 9LL</strong>.\nwhat3words <strong>[give the nearest one below]</strong>. [What happened], [how many hurt], [injuries].\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Location on farm</th>\n<th>what3words</th>\n<th>Link</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Farm entrance</strong></td>\n<td><code>///chicken.airless.hazy</code></td>\n<td>https://w3w.co/chicken.airless.hazy</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Truleigh Sands</strong></td>\n<td><code>///playback.poker.touched</code></td>\n<td>https://w3w.co/playback.poker.touched</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><em>Use the field name/number too (Module 16) so help comes to the right field. Mobile signal can be\npoor — </em><em>112</em><em> works on any network. Don't hang up until told to.</em></p>\n<hr />\n<h2>EMERGENCY / KEY CONTACTS</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Who</th>\n<th>Number</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Emergency services</strong></td>\n<td><strong>999</strong> (or 112)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Ben Hogan</strong></td>\n<td><strong>07876 683949</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Sandra Windus</strong></td>\n<td><strong>07901 521832</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Office</strong></td>\n<td><strong>01903 816065</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Electricity network (power line strike)</td>\n<td>105</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<hr />\n<h2>ADDRESS (for 999 / deliveries / visitors)</h2>\n<p><strong>Truleigh Manor Farm</strong>\n<strong>Edburton Road</strong>\n<strong>Henfield</strong>\n<strong>BN5 9LL</strong></p>\n<hr />\n<h2>ON-FARM (fill in at induction)</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th></th>\n<th></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Nearest first aid kit</td>\n<td><strong><em>_</em></strong><strong><em>_</em></strong><strong><em>_</em></strong>____</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Nearest fire extinguisher</td>\n<td><strong><em>_</em></strong><strong><em>_</em></strong><strong><em>_</em></strong>____</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Assembly point</td>\n<td><strong><em>_</em></strong><strong><em>_</em></strong><strong><em>_</em></strong>____</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Nearest defibrillator (if any)</td>\n<td><strong><em>_</em></strong><strong><em>_</em></strong><strong><em>_</em></strong>____</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Trained first aider on site</td>\n<td><strong><em>_</em></strong><strong><em>_</em></strong><strong><em>_</em></strong>____</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<hr />\n<p><strong>If someone is caught in machinery:</strong> Safe Stop / switch off power, call 999, control bleeding, keep\nany severed part clean &amp; cool with the casualty. <strong>Power line strike:</strong> stay in the cab, drive clear\nif you can, keep everyone back, call 999 + 105. <strong>Slurry gas / collapse:</strong> do NOT enter — call 999\nand keep clear.</p>"}, {"id": "sources", "group": "Reference", "short": "Sources & further reading", "title": "Sources &amp; Further Reading", "html": "<h1>Sources &amp; Further Reading</h1>\n<p>This training pack is built on current UK Health and Safety Executive (HSE) guidance and recognised agricultural good practice. The employer should use the original HSE materials alongside this pack and carry out a site-specific risk assessment.</p>\n<h2>Key UK law referenced</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Health and Safety at Work etc. Act 1974</li>\n<li>Management of Health and Safety at Work Regulations 1999 (regulation 19 — young persons)</li>\n<li>Prevention of Accidents to Children in Agriculture Regulations 1998</li>\n<li>Provision and Use of Work Equipment Regulations 1998 (PUWER)</li>\n<li>Lifting Operations and Lifting Equipment Regulations 1998 (LOLER)</li>\n<li>Reporting of Injuries, Diseases and Dangerous Occurrences Regulations 2013 (RIDDOR)</li>\n<li>Working Time Regulations 1998 (limits for under-18s)</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>HSE guidance (agriculture)</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>HSE — Children and public safety on farms: https://www.hse.gov.uk/agriculture/topics/children.htm</li>\n<li>HSE — Training, supervision and assessing risks to young people: https://www.hse.gov.uk/agriculture/experience/young-people-training.htm</li>\n<li>HSE — Preventing accidents to children on farms (INDG472): https://www.hse.gov.uk/pubns/indg472.htm</li>\n<li>HSE — Preventing accidents to children in agriculture (L116)</li>\n<li>HSE — Safe workplace transport in agriculture: https://www.hse.gov.uk/agriculture/topics/machinery/farm-vehicles-1.htm</li>\n<li>HSE — Safe maintenance / Safe Stop: https://www.hse.gov.uk/agriculture/topics/maintenance-1.htm</li>\n<li>HSE — Working safely with agricultural machinery (INDG241): https://www.hse.gov.uk/pubns/indg241.pdf</li>\n<li>HSE — Agriculture fatal injury statistics (annual)</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Other good practice</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>NFU — Safe Stop, know the procedure: https://www.nfuonline.com/updates-and-information/safe-stop-know-the-procedure/</li>\n<li>NFU Cymru — Driving tractors at age 16, the law: https://www.nfu-cymru.org.uk/news-and-information/driving-tractors-at-age-16-what-is-the-law/</li>\n<li>Farmers Weekly — Telehandler law: https://www.fwi.co.uk/machinery/tips-to-stay-on-the-right-side-of-telehandler-law</li>\n<li>Farmers Weekly — Tractors on the road, rules and regulations: https://www.fwi.co.uk/machinery/tractors-road-rights-wrongs-rules-regulations</li>\n<li>Round baler / bale handling safety (extension agricultural safety programmes)</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Machine specifications (for the Module 15 handbook)</h2>\n<p><em>Operating limits quoted in the handbook are indicative, from manufacturer/spec-sheet data; the\nmachine's own operator manual and load chart are always the authority.</em>\n- JCB 541-70 Loadall — LECTURA Specs: https://www.lectura-specs.com/en/model/forklifts/telehandlers-jcb/541-70-1048714\n- JCB 516-40 — LECTURA Specs: https://www.lectura-specs.com/en/model/forklifts/telehandlers-jcb/516-40-1160926 ; Ritchie Specs: https://www.ritchiespecs.com/model/jcb-516-40-telescopic-forklift\n- New Holland BB940A large square baler — Ritchie Specs: https://www.ritchiespecs.com/model/new-holland-bb940a-standard-baler\n- Case IH Puma CVX, John Deere 6R/6150R, Kuhn GF tedders / GA rakes / GMD disc mowers — manufacturer product pages and operator manuals\n- HSE — Lift trucks ACOP/guidance and PUWER/LOLER; HSE agriculture machinery pages (as above)</p>\n<h2>Farm data</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Field names, boundaries and map rendered from the farm's own KML (<code>Farm-Map/Truleigh_Manor_Farm_Field_Maps.kml</code>).</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Recommended accredited training providers</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Lantra</strong> (telehandler/RTITB, ATV, etc.)</li>\n<li><strong>City &amp; Guilds NPTC</strong></li>\n<li>Local agricultural colleges and approved instructors</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Guidance and statistics are updated periodically. Check the HSE agriculture pages for the latest version before relying on specific figures or rules.</p>\n</blockquote>"}];
window.MODULE_GROUPS = ["Start here", "Core safety", "Machinery handbook", "Reference"];
